{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01MfyMKCNAZ5rzSqNt76A9f5","type":"tool_result","content":"{\r\n  \"id\": \"US-013\",\r\n  \"title\": \"Create BEV C01-C02 Knowledge Assessments with Integrated Answer Schemes\",\r\n  \"priority\": \"high\",\r\n  \"description\": \"Develop Knowledge Assessment (KA) documents for BEV Competency Units 1-2 covering HV safety, PPE, battery fundamentals, BMS theory, and HV cable systems. Include multichoice, matching, and scenario-based question formats with complete answer keys aligned to CoCU assessment criteria.\",\r\n  \"acceptanceCriteria\": [\r\n    \"C01 KA.md contains 35+ questions (HV safety 40%, PPE 30%, battery basics 30%) with 25-mark answer key and assessment criteria references (e.g., 'CoCU C01.3.2 - HV Safety Procedures')\",\r\n    \"C02 KA.md contains 40+ questions (BMS theory 35%, HV cable systems 35%, OBC 30%) with 25-mark answer key and detailed explanations showing required technical depth\",\r\n    \"Minimum 30% scenario-based questions across both files with sample answers of 50+ words demonstrating expected technical knowledge for Level 3 qualification\"\r\n  ],\r\n  \"technicalNotes\": [\r\n    \"File to create: bev-diagnostic-rectification/CU-01/KA.md and CU-02/KA.md (follow JPK Buku Panduan WIM 2020 format with question types clearly labeled)\",\r\n    \"Validation: grep 'CoCU C0[12]' bev-diagnostic-rectification/CU-0[12]/KA.md to verify all questions tagged with CoCU reference codes\",\r\n    \"Test: wc -l bev-diagnostic-rectification/CU-0[12]/KA.md and count questions via regex 'Q[0-9]+\\\\.' to confirm 35-40 question count minimum\"\r\n  ],\r\n  \"dependencies\": [],\r\n  \"estimatedComplexity\": \"small\",\r\n  \"passes\": false,\r\n  \"_source\": \"ai-example\",\r\n  \"model\": \"sonnet\",\r\n  \"last_attempted\": \"2026-04-16T14:48:06Z\"\r\n}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4aad63e8-74bd-4073-a099-f779e2e73025","uuid":"9bb296a3-3463-437a-9657-3e33ee63ab31","timestamp":"2026-04-16T14:48:42.131Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"{\r\n  \"id\": \"US-013\",\r\n  \"title\": \"Create BEV C01-C02 Knowledge Assessments with Integrated Answer Schemes\",\r\n  \"priority\": \"high\",\r\n  \"description\": \"Develop Knowledge Assessment (KA) documents for BEV Competency Units 1-2 covering HV safety, PPE, battery fundamentals, BMS theory, and HV cable systems. Include multichoice, matching, and scenario-based question formats with complete answer keys aligned to CoCU assessment criteria.\",\r\n  \"acceptanceCriteria\": [\r\n    \"C01 KA.md contains 35+ questions (HV safety 40%, PPE 30%, battery basics 30%) with 25-mark answer key and assessment criteria references (e.g., 'CoCU C01.3.2 - HV Safety Procedures')\",\r\n    \"C02 KA.md contains 40+ questions (BMS theory 35%, HV cable systems 35%, OBC 30%) with 25-mark answer key and detailed explanations showing required technical depth\",\r\n    \"Minimum 30% scenario-based questions across both files with sample answers of 50+ words demonstrating expected technical knowledge for Level 3 qualification\"\r\n  ],\r\n  \"technicalNotes\": [\r\n    \"File to create: bev-diagnostic-rectification/CU-01/KA.md and CU-02/KA.md (follow JPK Buku Panduan WIM 2020 format with question types clearly labeled)\",\r\n    \"Validation: grep 'CoCU C0[12]' bev-diagnostic-rectification/CU-0[12]/KA.md to verify all questions tagged with CoCU reference codes\",\r\n    \"Test: wc -l bev-diagnostic-rectification/CU-0[12]/KA.md and count questions via regex 'Q[0-9]+\\\\.' to confirm 35-40 question count minimum\"\r\n  ],\r\n  \"dependencies\": [],\r\n  \"estimatedComplexity\": \"small\",\r\n  \"passes\": false,\r\n  \"_source\": \"ai-example\",\r\n  \"model\": \"sonnet\",\r\n  \"last_attempted\": \"2026-04-16T14:48:06Z\"\r\n}","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PUaDucb5DMNAieweoboRPA","type":"tool_result","content":"### What was implemented\r\n- C01/KK-01, KK-02, KK-03: Fixed safety glasses spec from DIN EN166 to MS ISO 16321 / ANSI Z87.1 (Malaysian standard)\r\n- C02/KK-01: Expanded inline equipment text to tables; expanded placeholder Step 2.1-2.5; restructured checklist to 5-domain structure with Remarks column\r\n- C02/KK-02: Expanded inline equipment text to tables; added numbered safety precautions list; restructured checklist to 5 domains\r\n- C02/KK-03: Expanded inline equipment text to tables; fixed AMARAN: format; added numbered safety precautions; restructured checklist to 5 domains\r\n\r\n### Patterns discovered\r\n- C01 KK files were well-structured but had DIN EN166 glasses spec error carried over from KP files\r\n- C02 KK files used compact inline lists instead of tables at initial creation\r\n- 5-domain checklist structure (PROCESS/OUTPUT/ATTITUDE/SAFETY/ENVIRONMENT) from C01 is the standard for all KK files\r\n- Assessment checklist requires Remarks column (5 columns total)\r\n\r\n### Gotchas\r\n- C02/KK-03 used AMARAN: without the / WARNING: bilingual suffix — always use AMARAN / WARNING: format\r\n- C02/KK-01 Step 2 was a placeholder referencing another file — must always expand placeholders before submission\r\n- Coolant in OBC work is SW305 scheduled waste — must appear in both safety precautions and ENVIRONMENT domain of checklist\r\n## Iteration 1 - Thu, Apr 16, 2026  9:38:23 PM\nCompleted: Enhance BEV KK practical procedures — step-by-step accuracy and safety checklists (ID: US-005) in 9m\n\n\n## Pre-analysis for US-007 (Gemini 2.5 Pro)\nHere is the technical brief for user story US-007.\n\n**Subject: Technical Brief for US-007: Enhance Aesthetic KPs**\n\nThis story is a content enhancement task, not a Python coding task. The agent will review and update existing Markdown knowledge package (KP) files.\n\n**1. Target Files (Markdown, not Python):**\n- `aesthetic-services/C01/KP-01.md` through `KP-05.md`\n- `aesthetic-services/C02/KP-01.md` through `KP-07.md`\n\n**2. Frappe DocTypes/Hooks:**\n- No direct interaction with DocTypes or hooks is expected. The task is confined to updating the content of the specified `.md` files.\n\n**3. Recommended Implementation Approach:**\n- Read each target `.md` file.\n- Review content against acceptance criteria:\n    - C01: Correctness of consultation techniques, BMI calculation, body typing.\n    - C02: Accuracy of thermal therapy details, safety, and contraindications.\n- Enhance the text for anatomical and physiological accuracy.\n- Ensure all key concepts include required bilingual (English/local language) terminology.\n- Overwrite the existing files with the updated, reviewed content.\n\n**4. Edge Cases & Gotchas:**\n- **Bilingual Accuracy:** This is a critical requirement. The agent must provide correct translations for technical terms.\n- **Subject-Matter Expertise:** The content is technical. The agent must ensure the information on anatomy, BMI, and thermal therapies is accurate and up-to-date.\n\n\r\n## Story US-007 — Enhance Aesthetic C01-C02 KP — body consultation and thermal therapy theory\r\n\r\n### What was implemented\r\n- C01/KP-01: Added AMARAN/WARNING callout for disinfectant chemical hazards; enhanced magnifying lamp usage detail; added flexible measuring tape to tools table\r\n- C01/KP-03: Added AMARAN/WARNING callout before contraindications section; added calliper skinfold measurement sites table (4 sites with location + client position); added Waist-Hip Ratio (WHR) section with formula and risk classification table; renumbered 9.3 → 9.4 for Limitations\r\n- C02/KP-01: Added AMARAN/WARNING callout before contraindications section; added thermal therapy modalities overview table with bilingual terms (6 modalities including body wrap)\r\n- C02/KP-02: Added AMARAN/WARNING callout for electrical safety in thermal equipment room\r\n- C02/KP-05: Added body wrap (balutan badan) as modality 2.6 with full parameter table, procedure overview, and product types table; restructured section 4.0 with per-modality contraindications table and general precautions subsections\r\n\r\n### Patterns discovered\r\n- AMARAN / WARNING callout pattern established for BEV files applies identically to Aesthetic KP files\r\n- Body wrap was a critical missing modality — referenced in C01/KP-03 recommended treatments but not described in C02/KP-05 until this story\r\n- WHR (Waist-Hip Ratio) is the standard supplementary measure alongside BMI for body consultation — WHO cut-offs: women >0.85 = high risk, men >0.95 = high risk\r\n- Per-modality contraindication tables add significant value to C02/KP-05 — each modality has unique specific contraindications beyond the general list in C02/KP-01\r\n\r\n### Gotchas\r\n- C01/KP-03 section 9.3 needed renumbering to 9.4 after inserting WHR as new 9.3\r\n- Body wrap uses occlusive heat (no external heat source) — different physics from all other modalities\r\n- Calliper sites: 4 standard sites (triceps, subscapular, suprailiac, abdominal) — positions matter for accurate measurement\r\n\n## Story US-008 — Enhance Aesthetic C03 KP — manual body massage techniques and anatomy\n\n### What was implemented\n- KP-01: Added AMARAN/WARNING callout before contraindications; restructured into local/systemic/relative categories; expanded absolute contraindications (added anticoagulants, alcohol intoxication, psychosis, local contraindications section)\n- KP-02: Added AMARAN/WARNING callout for massage medium allergy risk (nut-based carriers)\n- KP-03: Added AMARAN/WARNING callout for professional draping standards\n- KP-04: Added AMARAN/WARNING callout; enhanced all 5 classical movements with hand position detail and tapotement sub-type table; added anatomy section 8.0 (major muscle groups by region, physiological effects table, anatomical landmarks to avoid)\n- KP-05: Added AMARAN/WARNING callout for post-massage medical referral\n- KP-06: Added AMARAN/WARNING callout for massage medium contamination prevention\n\n### Patterns discovered\n- Contraindications for massage should be split 3 ways: systemic/absolute, local (area-avoid), relative — clearer than just absolute/relative\n- Tapotement has 5 sub-types with different hand positions — a table is the clearest format\n- Anatomy section belongs in KP-04 (the \"carry out\" KP) not KP-01 (consultation)\n- Anatomical landmarks to avoid (popliteal fossa, femoral triangle, carotid triangle, kidney region) are distinct from contraindications — warrant their own table\n\n### Gotchas\n- Friction technique requires REMOVING medium first — the \"dry skin\" caution is critical to its effectiveness\n- Vibration is neurological (light pressure), not mechanical — different from all other movements\n- Anticoagulant medications (warfarin, heparin) are an absolute contraindication often missing from standard lists\n## Iteration 1 - Thu, Apr 16, 2026  9:59:53 PM\nCompleted: Enhance Aesthetic C03 KP — manual body massage techniques and anatomy (ID: US-008) in 9m\n\n\n## Story US-009 — Enhance Aesthetic C04 KP — electrotherapy modalities (6 types) with safety\n\n### What was implemented\n- KP-01: Added AMARAN/WARNING for electrical safety in work area (mains electricity, wet hands, damaged cables)\n- KP-02: Added AMARAN/WARNING before contraindications; expanded modality-specific table to include all 6 modalities including mechanical/gyratory (previously missing); added acute inflammation/fever to absolute list; enhanced reason descriptions\n- KP-03: Added AMARAN/WARNING with specific placements to avoid (thorax, carotid, cranium); added modality-specific contraindications at end of precautions\n- KP-04: Added AMARAN/WARNING for galvanic shock risk; added full procedure overview (was completely missing — only had precautions); added modality-specific contraindications\n- KP-05: Added AMARAN/WARNING for varicose vein risk; added lymph node drainage direction table (5 body areas); enhanced precautions with specific cup release technique; added modality-specific contraindications\n- KP-06: Added AMARAN/WARNING for stationary rotating head risk; enhanced precautions; added machine specifications table (motor type, RPM range, attachment type); added modality-specific contraindications\n- KP-07: Added AMARAN/WARNING for flammable products fire risk; added treatment parameters table comparing direct vs indirect method (was missing — only had precautions); enhanced precautions; added modality-specific contraindications\n- KP-08: Added AMARAN/WARNING for pacemaker risk even at sub-sensory levels; enhanced contraindications (added phlebitis, recent surgery); added machine specifications table; added note on dedicated micro-current gel requirement\n- KP-09: Added AMARAN/WARNING for adverse reaction referral protocol; enhanced aftercare table with specific durations; added modality-specific aftercare notes table (5 modalities)\n- KP-10: Added AMARAN/WARNING for clinical waste classification; restructured waste disposal into a classification table with 7 waste types; added inventory management section with FIFO and stock count procedures\n\n### Patterns discovered\n- KP-04 (Galvanic) was missing a procedure overview entirely — all other modality KPs had one. Galvanic is unique in requiring polarity check before product application\n- KP-07 (High Frequency) was missing a parameters table — critical because direct and indirect methods have completely different applications, yet shared the same machine output\n- Mechanical/gyratory was missing from the modality-specific contraindications table in KP-02 — the 6th modality was not represented\n- Micro-current requires dedicated conductive gel — not standard conductive gel — a frequently missed clinical detail\n- High frequency fire hazard from flammable products is a critical safety point often understated in training materials\n\n### Gotchas\n- Galvanic shock (from moving electrode without zeroing current) is distinct from electrical burns — it is a sharp pain event caused by sudden current change\n- Vacuum suction stationary cup → petechiae (pin-point bruising) is the most common client complaint — must be explained in aftercare\n- High frequency direct method: electrode on skin BEFORE switching on (not after) — prevents sparking on initial contact\n- Micro-current: pacemaker contraindication applies even at sub-sensory levels because demand-type pacemakers can interpret micro-currents as cardiac signals\n## Iteration 1 - Thu, Apr 16, 2026 10:17:39 PM\nCompleted: Enhance Aesthetic C04 KP — electrotherapy modalities (6 types) with safety (ID: US-009) in 10m\n\n\n## Pre-analysis for US-012 (Gemini 2.5 Pro)\nThis technical brief is for a Claude Code agent tasked with implementing user story US-012 within the `lhdn_payroll_integration` Frappe app.\n\n**Objective:** Implement a validation system to ensure \"Aesthetic KK\" procedure documents, which are referenced by a Frappe DocType, contain all required sections and details.\n\n**1. Python Files to Modify:**\n*   `lhdn_payroll_integration/hooks.py`: To hook the validation logic into a DocType event.\n*   `[app]/[doctype]/[doctype].py`: The controller file for the DocType managing the procedures (e.g., `Aesthetic Procedure`). This will house the main validation method.\n*   A new utility file (e.g., `lhdn_payroll_integration/utils/validator.py`) could be created to contain the specific Markdown parsing and checking logic, keeping the controller lean.\n\n**2. Frappe DocTypes & Hooks:**\n*   **DocType**: The primary target is a DocType that contains a field linking to the `.md` procedure files. The name is likely `Aesthetic Procedure` or similar.\n*   **Hook**: Use the `validate` document event in `hooks.py`. This will automatically trigger the validation logic every time a document of that type is saved, preventing non-compliant entries.\n    *   e.g., `doc_events = {\"Aesthetic Procedure\": {\"validate\": \"path.to.validation.method\"}}`\n\n**3. Recommended Implementation Approach:**\n1.  In the target DocType's Python controller, define a method (e.g., `validate_procedure_file`).\n2.  This method should read the file path from the document's field.\n3.  It will then open and parse the content of the Markdown file.\n4.  Check for the existence of required sections (Client Preparation, Hygiene, Sterilization, PPE, Assessment Checklist) using regex or a parsing library.\n5.  If a section is missing or incomplete, raise `frappe.ValidationError` with a clear message indicating the deficiency.\n\n**4. Edge Cases & Gotchas:**\n*   The validation logic must handle variations in Markdown formatting (e.g., different heading styles).\n*   Implement `try...except` blocks to handle `IOError` for missing files or incorrect paths.\n*   For a better user experience on existing documents, consider adding a custom button to the DocType form that triggers the validation manually, rather than only blocking on save.\n\n\n## Pre-analysis for US-012 (Gemini 2.5 Pro)\nThis technical brief is for a Claude Code agent tasked with implementing user story US-012 within the `lhdn_payroll_integration` Frappe app.\r\n\r\n**Objective:** Implement a validation system to ensure \"Aesthetic KK\" procedure documents, which are referenced by a Frappe DocType, contain all required sections and details.\r\n\r\n**1. Python Files to Modify:**\r\n*   `lhdn_payroll_integration/hooks.py`: To hook the validation logic into a DocType event.\r\n*   `[app]/[doctype]/[doctype].py`: The controller file for the DocType managing the procedures (e.g., `Aesthetic Procedure`). This will house the main validation method.\r\n*   A new utility file (e.g., `lhdn_payroll_integration/utils/validator.py`) could be created to contain the specific Markdown parsing and checking logic, keeping the controller lean.\r\n\r\n**2. Frappe DocTypes & Hooks:**\r\n*   **DocType**: The primary target is a DocType that contains a field linking to the `.md` procedure files. The name is likely `Aesthetic Procedure` or similar.\r\n*   **Hook**: Use the `validate` document event in `hooks.py`. This will automatically trigger the validation logic every time a document of that type is saved, preventing non-compliant entries.\r\n    *   e.g., `doc_events = {\"Aesthetic Procedure\": {\"validate\": \"path.to.validation.method\"}}`\r\n\r\n**3. Recommended Implementation Approach:**\r\n1.  In the target DocType's Python controller, define a method (e.g., `validate_procedure_file`).\r\n2.  This method should read the file path from the document's field.\r\n3.  It will then open and parse the content of the Markdown file.\r\n4.  Check for the existence of required sections (Client Preparation, Hygiene, Sterilization, PPE, Assessment Checklist) using regex or a parsing library.\r\n5.  If a section is missing or incomplete, raise `frappe.ValidationError` with a clear message indicating the deficiency.\r\n\r\n**4. Edge Cases & Gotchas:**\r\n*   The validation logic must handle variations in Markdown formatting (e.g., different heading styles).\r\n*   Implement `try...except` blocks to handle `IOError` for missing files or incorrect paths.\r\n*   For a better user experience on existing documents, consider adding a custom button to the DocType form that triggers the validation manually, rather than only blocking on save.\n\n\n## Story US-012 — Enhance Aesthetic KK practical procedures — treatment techniques and hygiene\n\n### What was implemented\n- C01/KK-01: Added Step 1.5 (inspect client gown/slippers for cleanliness/damage); changed surface disinfectant spec to QAC with contact time; added \"ONE DIRECTION\" wipe to Step 2.2; expanded checklist from 15 to 16 items with new PROCESS item 5 for client items check\n- C02/KK-01: Rewrote Step 1 (1.1–1.6): added PPE donning, QAC + one-direction wipe + air dry, instrument cleaning (BP monitor with 70% IPA), client identity verification, gown provision for physical assessment; expanded checklist from 14 to 17 items with 2 SAFETY items\n- C03/KK-01: Added QAC spec + contact time to Step 2.1; added \"ONE DIRECTION only\" and discard cloth to Step 2.2; added Step 2.3 (clipboard/pen cleaned with 70% IPA); added allergy confirmation as SAFETY item 14 in checklist; expanded from 14 to 15 items\n- C04/KK-01: Added Step 6.6 (pre-treatment metal jewellery removal notice to client); added new Step 7 \"Pre-Client Entry Verification\" (5 sub-steps: identity check, jewellery/implant verbal confirmation, changing, positioning, draping); renamed old Step 7 to Step 8; expanded checklist from 15 to 20 items with 3 new PROCESS items (8–11) and 1 new SAFETY item (18)\n\n### Patterns discovered\n- QAC (quaternary ammonium compound) is the standard surface disinfectant; 70% IPA is the standard for instruments/equipment surfaces — always distinguish these in hygiene steps\n- \"ONE DIRECTION\" wipe is a hygiene principle — critical to include explicitly, not just implied\n- Disinfectant contact time (label time, typically 60 seconds) must be observed — wipe immediately defeats the purpose\n- For electrotherapy KK-01 (work area prep), the pre-treatment client safety check (jewellery, implants, pacemaker) belongs at the END of this KK as the final gate before treatment starts\n\n### Gotchas\n- C04/KK-01 was missing the critical pre-electrotherapy client preparation entirely — metal jewellery on a client during electrical treatment is a serious hazard\n- C02/KK-01 had only 1 SAFETY domain item — always target minimum 2 SAFETY items per checklist\n- C03/KK-01 allergy confirmation (nut-based carrier oils) was in the procedure steps but NOT in the checklist — always cross-check that checklist items map to critical procedure steps\n## Iteration 1 - Thu, Apr 16, 2026 10:36:31 PM\nCompleted: Enhance Aesthetic KK practical procedures — treatment techniques and hygiene (ID: US-012) in 7m","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4aad63e8-74bd-4073-a099-f779e2e73025","uuid":"526f4ddc-b554-4485-a374-58ef78531a4a","timestamp":"2026-04-16T14:48:46.857Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"### What was implemented\r\n- C01/KK-01, KK-02, KK-03: Fixed safety glasses spec from DIN EN166 to MS ISO 16321 / ANSI Z87.1 (Malaysian standard)\r\n- C02/KK-01: Expanded inline equipment text to tables; expanded placeholder Step 2.1-2.5; restructured checklist to 5-domain structure with Remarks column\r\n- C02/KK-02: Expanded inline equipment text to tables; added numbered safety precautions list; restructured checklist to 5 domains\r\n- C02/KK-03: Expanded inline equipment text to tables; fixed AMARAN: format; added numbered safety precautions; restructured checklist to 5 domains\r\n\r\n### Patterns discovered\r\n- C01 KK files were well-structured but had DIN EN166 glasses spec error carried over from KP files\r\n- C02 KK files used compact inline lists instead of tables at initial creation\r\n- 5-domain checklist structure (PROCESS/OUTPUT/ATTITUDE/SAFETY/ENVIRONMENT) from C01 is the standard for all KK files\r\n- Assessment checklist requires Remarks column (5 columns total)\r\n\r\n### Gotchas\r\n- C02/KK-03 used AMARAN: without the / WARNING: bilingual suffix — always use AMARAN / WARNING: format\r\n- C02/KK-01 Step 2 was a placeholder referencing another file — must always expand placeholders before submission\r\n- Coolant in OBC work is SW305 scheduled waste — must appear in both safety precautions and ENVIRONMENT domain of checklist\r\n## Iteration 1 - Thu, Apr 16, 2026  9:38:23 PM\nCompleted: Enhance BEV KK practical procedures — step-by-step accuracy and safety checklists (ID: US-005) in 9m\n\n\n## Pre-analysis for US-007 (Gemini 2.5 Pro)\nHere is the technical brief for user story US-007.\n\n**Subject: Technical Brief for US-007: Enhance Aesthetic KPs**\n\nThis story is a content enhancement task, not a Python coding task. The agent will review and update existing Markdown knowledge package (KP) files.\n\n**1. Target Files (Markdown, not Python):**\n- `aesthetic-services/C01/KP-01.md` through `KP-05.md`\n- `aesthetic-services/C02/KP-01.md` through `KP-07.md`\n\n**2. Frappe DocTypes/Hooks:**\n- No direct interaction with DocTypes or hooks is expected. The task is confined to updating the content of the specified `.md` files.\n\n**3. Recommended Implementation Approach:**\n- Read each target `.md` file.\n- Review content against acceptance criteria:\n    - C01: Correctness of consultation techniques, BMI calculation, body typing.\n    - C02: Accuracy of thermal therapy details, safety, and contraindications.\n- Enhance the text for anatomical and physiological accuracy.\n- Ensure all key concepts include required bilingual (English/local language) terminology.\n- Overwrite the existing files with the updated, reviewed content.\n\n**4. Edge Cases & Gotchas:**\n- **Bilingual Accuracy:** This is a critical requirement. The agent must provide correct translations for technical terms.\n- **Subject-Matter Expertise:** The content is technical. The agent must ensure the information on anatomy, BMI, and thermal therapies is accurate and up-to-date.\n\n\r\n## Story US-007 — Enhance Aesthetic C01-C02 KP — body consultation and thermal therapy theory\r\n\r\n### What was implemented\r\n- C01/KP-01: Added AMARAN/WARNING callout for disinfectant chemical hazards; enhanced magnifying lamp usage detail; added flexible measuring tape to tools table\r\n- C01/KP-03: Added AMARAN/WARNING callout before contraindications section; added calliper skinfold measurement sites table (4 sites with location + client position); added Waist-Hip Ratio (WHR) section with formula and risk classification table; renumbered 9.3 → 9.4 for Limitations\r\n- C02/KP-01: Added AMARAN/WARNING callout before contraindications section; added thermal therapy modalities overview table with bilingual terms (6 modalities including body wrap)\r\n- C02/KP-02: Added AMARAN/WARNING callout for electrical safety in thermal equipment room\r\n- C02/KP-05: Added body wrap (balutan badan) as modality 2.6 with full parameter table, procedure overview, and product types table; restructured section 4.0 with per-modality contraindications table and general precautions subsections\r\n\r\n### Patterns discovered\r\n- AMARAN / WARNING callout pattern established for BEV files applies identically to Aesthetic KP files\r\n- Body wrap was a critical missing modality — referenced in C01/KP-03 recommended treatments but not described in C02/KP-05 until this story\r\n- WHR (Waist-Hip Ratio) is the standard supplementary measure alongside BMI for body consultation — WHO cut-offs: women >0.85 = high risk, men >0.95 = high risk\r\n- Per-modality contraindication tables add significant value to C02/KP-05 — each modality has unique specific contraindications beyond the general list in C02/KP-01\r\n\r\n### Gotchas\r\n- C01/KP-03 section 9.3 needed renumbering to 9.4 after inserting WHR as new 9.3\r\n- Body wrap uses occlusive heat (no external heat source) — different physics from all other modalities\r\n- Calliper sites: 4 standard sites (triceps, subscapular, suprailiac, abdominal) — positions matter for accurate measurement\r\n\n## Story US-008 — Enhance Aesthetic C03 KP — manual body massage techniques and anatomy\n\n### What was implemented\n- KP-01: Added AMARAN/WARNING callout before contraindications; restructured into local/systemic/relative categories; expanded absolute contraindications (added anticoagulants, alcohol intoxication, psychosis, local contraindications section)\n- KP-02: Added AMARAN/WARNING callout for massage medium allergy risk (nut-based carriers)\n- KP-03: Added AMARAN/WARNING callout for professional draping standards\n- KP-04: Added AMARAN/WARNING callout; enhanced all 5 classical movements with hand position detail and tapotement sub-type table; added anatomy section 8.0 (major muscle groups by region, physiological effects table, anatomical landmarks to avoid)\n- KP-05: Added AMARAN/WARNING callout for post-massage medical referral\n- KP-06: Added AMARAN/WARNING callout for massage medium contamination prevention\n\n### Patterns discovered\n- Contraindications for massage should be split 3 ways: systemic/absolute, local (area-avoid), relative — clearer than just absolute/relative\n- Tapotement has 5 sub-types with different hand positions — a table is the clearest format\n- Anatomy section belongs in KP-04 (the \"carry out\" KP) not KP-01 (consultation)\n- Anatomical landmarks to avoid (popliteal fossa, femoral triangle, carotid triangle, kidney region) are distinct from contraindications — warrant their own table\n\n### Gotchas\n- Friction technique requires REMOVING medium first — the \"dry skin\" caution is critical to its effectiveness\n- Vibration is neurological (light pressure), not mechanical — different from all other movements\n- Anticoagulant medications (warfarin, heparin) are an absolute contraindication often missing from standard lists\n## Iteration 1 - Thu, Apr 16, 2026  9:59:53 PM\nCompleted: Enhance Aesthetic C03 KP — manual body massage techniques and anatomy (ID: US-008) in 9m\n\n\n## Story US-009 — Enhance Aesthetic C04 KP — electrotherapy modalities (6 types) with safety\n\n### What was implemented\n- KP-01: Added AMARAN/WARNING for electrical safety in work area (mains electricity, wet hands, damaged cables)\n- KP-02: Added AMARAN/WARNING before contraindications; expanded modality-specific table to include all 6 modalities including mechanical/gyratory (previously missing); added acute inflammation/fever to absolute list; enhanced reason descriptions\n- KP-03: Added AMARAN/WARNING with specific placements to avoid (thorax, carotid, cranium); added modality-specific contraindications at end of precautions\n- KP-04: Added AMARAN/WARNING for galvanic shock risk; added full procedure overview (was completely missing — only had precautions); added modality-specific contraindications\n- KP-05: Added AMARAN/WARNING for varicose vein risk; added lymph node drainage direction table (5 body areas); enhanced precautions with specific cup release technique; added modality-specific contraindications\n- KP-06: Added AMARAN/WARNING for stationary rotating head risk; enhanced precautions; added machine specifications table (motor type, RPM range, attachment type); added modality-specific contraindications\n- KP-07: Added AMARAN/WARNING for flammable products fire risk; added treatment parameters table comparing direct vs indirect method (was missing — only had precautions); enhanced precautions; added modality-specific contraindications\n- KP-08: Added AMARAN/WARNING for pacemaker risk even at sub-sensory levels; enhanced contraindications (added phlebitis, recent surgery); added machine specifications table; added note on dedicated micro-current gel requirement\n- KP-09: Added AMARAN/WARNING for adverse reaction referral protocol; enhanced aftercare table with specific durations; added modality-specific aftercare notes table (5 modalities)\n- KP-10: Added AMARAN/WARNING for clinical waste classification; restructured waste disposal into a classification table with 7 waste types; added inventory management section with FIFO and stock count procedures\n\n### Patterns discovered\n- KP-04 (Galvanic) was missing a procedure overview entirely — all other modality KPs had one. Galvanic is unique in requiring polarity check before product application\n- KP-07 (High Frequency) was missing a parameters table — critical because direct and indirect methods have completely different applications, yet shared the same machine output\n- Mechanical/gyratory was missing from the modality-specific contraindications table in KP-02 — the 6th modality was not represented\n- Micro-current requires dedicated conductive gel — not standard conductive gel — a frequently missed clinical detail\n- High frequency fire hazard from flammable products is a critical safety point often understated in training materials\n\n### Gotchas\n- Galvanic shock (from moving electrode without zeroing current) is distinct from electrical burns — it is a sharp pain event caused by sudden current change\n- Vacuum suction stationary cup → petechiae (pin-point bruising) is the most common client complaint — must be explained in aftercare\n- High frequency direct method: electrode on skin BEFORE switching on (not after) — prevents sparking on initial contact\n- Micro-current: pacemaker contraindication applies even at sub-sensory levels because demand-type pacemakers can interpret micro-currents as cardiac signals\n## Iteration 1 - Thu, Apr 16, 2026 10:17:39 PM\nCompleted: Enhance Aesthetic C04 KP — electrotherapy modalities (6 types) with safety (ID: US-009) in 10m\n\n\n## Pre-analysis for US-012 (Gemini 2.5 Pro)\nThis technical brief is for a Claude Code agent tasked with implementing user story US-012 within the `lhdn_payroll_integration` Frappe app.\n\n**Objective:** Implement a validation system to ensure \"Aesthetic KK\" procedure documents, which are referenced by a Frappe DocType, contain all required sections and details.\n\n**1. Python Files to Modify:**\n*   `lhdn_payroll_integration/hooks.py`: To hook the validation logic into a DocType event.\n*   `[app]/[doctype]/[doctype].py`: The controller file for the DocType managing the procedures (e.g., `Aesthetic Procedure`). This will house the main validation method.\n*   A new utility file (e.g., `lhdn_payroll_integration/utils/validator.py`) could be created to contain the specific Markdown parsing and checking logic, keeping the controller lean.\n\n**2. Frappe DocTypes & Hooks:**\n*   **DocType**: The primary target is a DocType that contains a field linking to the `.md` procedure files. The name is likely `Aesthetic Procedure` or similar.\n*   **Hook**: Use the `validate` document event in `hooks.py`. This will automatically trigger the validation logic every time a document of that type is saved, preventing non-compliant entries.\n    *   e.g., `doc_events = {\"Aesthetic Procedure\": {\"validate\": \"path.to.validation.method\"}}`\n\n**3. Recommended Implementation Approach:**\n1.  In the target DocType's Python controller, define a method (e.g., `validate_procedure_file`).\n2.  This method should read the file path from the document's field.\n3.  It will then open and parse the content of the Markdown file.\n4.  Check for the existence of required sections (Client Preparation, Hygiene, Sterilization, PPE, Assessment Checklist) using regex or a parsing library.\n5.  If a section is missing or incomplete, raise `frappe.ValidationError` with a clear message indicating the deficiency.\n\n**4. Edge Cases & Gotchas:**\n*   The validation logic must handle variations in Markdown formatting (e.g., different heading styles).\n*   Implement `try...except` blocks to handle `IOError` for missing files or incorrect paths.\n*   For a better user experience on existing documents, consider adding a custom button to the DocType form that triggers the validation manually, rather than only blocking on save.\n\n\n## Pre-analysis for US-012 (Gemini 2.5 Pro)\nThis technical brief is for a Claude Code agent tasked with implementing user story US-012 within the `lhdn_payroll_integration` Frappe app.\r\n\r\n**Objective:** Implement a validation system to ensure \"Aesthetic KK\" procedure documents, which are referenced by a Frappe DocType, contain all required sections and details.\r\n\r\n**1. Python Files to Modify:**\r\n*   `lhdn_payroll_integration/hooks.py`: To hook the validation logic into a DocType event.\r\n*   `[app]/[doctype]/[doctype].py`: The controller file for the DocType managing the procedures (e.g., `Aesthetic Procedure`). This will house the main validation method.\r\n*   A new utility file (e.g., `lhdn_payroll_integration/utils/validator.py`) could be created to contain the specific Markdown parsing and checking logic, keeping the controller lean.\r\n\r\n**2. Frappe DocTypes & Hooks:**\r\n*   **DocType**: The primary target is a DocType that contains a field linking to the `.md` procedure files. The name is likely `Aesthetic Procedure` or similar.\r\n*   **Hook**: Use the `validate` document event in `hooks.py`. This will automatically trigger the validation logic every time a document of that type is saved, preventing non-compliant entries.\r\n    *   e.g., `doc_events = {\"Aesthetic Procedure\": {\"validate\": \"path.to.validation.method\"}}`\r\n\r\n**3. Recommended Implementation Approach:**\r\n1.  In the target DocType's Python controller, define a method (e.g., `validate_procedure_file`).\r\n2.  This method should read the file path from the document's field.\r\n3.  It will then open and parse the content of the Markdown file.\r\n4.  Check for the existence of required sections (Client Preparation, Hygiene, Sterilization, PPE, Assessment Checklist) using regex or a parsing library.\r\n5.  If a section is missing or incomplete, raise `frappe.ValidationError` with a clear message indicating the deficiency.\r\n\r\n**4. Edge Cases & Gotchas:**\r\n*   The validation logic must handle variations in Markdown formatting (e.g., different heading styles).\r\n*   Implement `try...except` blocks to handle `IOError` for missing files or incorrect paths.\r\n*   For a better user experience on existing documents, consider adding a custom button to the DocType form that triggers the validation manually, rather than only blocking on save.\n\n\n## Story US-012 — Enhance Aesthetic KK practical procedures — treatment techniques and hygiene\n\n### What was implemented\n- C01/KK-01: Added Step 1.5 (inspect client gown/slippers for cleanliness/damage); changed surface disinfectant spec to QAC with contact time; added \"ONE DIRECTION\" wipe to Step 2.2; expanded checklist from 15 to 16 items with new PROCESS item 5 for client items check\n- C02/KK-01: Rewrote Step 1 (1.1–1.6): added PPE donning, QAC + one-direction wipe + air dry, instrument cleaning (BP monitor with 70% IPA), client identity verification, gown provision for physical assessment; expanded checklist from 14 to 17 items with 2 SAFETY items\n- C03/KK-01: Added QAC spec + contact time to Step 2.1; added \"ONE DIRECTION only\" and discard cloth to Step 2.2; added Step 2.3 (clipboard/pen cleaned with 70% IPA); added allergy confirmation as SAFETY item 14 in checklist; expanded from 14 to 15 items\n- C04/KK-01: Added Step 6.6 (pre-treatment metal jewellery removal notice to client); added new Step 7 \"Pre-Client Entry Verification\" (5 sub-steps: identity check, jewellery/implant verbal confirmation, changing, positioning, draping); renamed old Step 7 to Step 8; expanded checklist from 15 to 20 items with 3 new PROCESS items (8–11) and 1 new SAFETY item (18)\n\n### Patterns discovered\n- QAC (quaternary ammonium compound) is the standard surface disinfectant; 70% IPA is the standard for instruments/equipment surfaces — always distinguish these in hygiene steps\n- \"ONE DIRECTION\" wipe is a hygiene principle — critical to include explicitly, not just implied\n- Disinfectant contact time (label time, typically 60 seconds) must be observed — wipe immediately defeats the purpose\n- For electrotherapy KK-01 (work area prep), the pre-treatment client safety check (jewellery, implants, pacemaker) belongs at the END of this KK as the final gate before treatment starts\n\n### Gotchas\n- C04/KK-01 was missing the critical pre-electrotherapy client preparation entirely — metal jewellery on a client during electrical treatment is a serious hazard\n- C02/KK-01 had only 1 SAFETY domain item — always target minimum 2 SAFETY items per checklist\n- C03/KK-01 allergy confirmation (nut-based carrier oils) was in the procedure steps but NOT in the checklist — always cross-check that checklist items map to critical procedure steps\n## Iteration 1 - Thu, Apr 16, 2026 10:36:31 PM\nCompleted: Enhance Aesthetic KK practical procedures — treatment techniques and hygiene (ID: US-012) in 7m","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
