{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01P26Lpvp5xy5EsN2qRFMB4j","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# NOSS Extract: G452-010-3:2023 Battery Electric Vehicle (BEV) Diagnostic and Rectification\n2\t\n3\t> **Source:** G452-010-3:2023 Battery Electric Vehicle (BEV) Diagnostic and Rectification (138 pages)\n4\t> **Level:** 3\n5\t> **Extracted:** 2026-04-16\n6\t> **Purpose:** Source-of-truth for WIM document generation\n7\t\n8\t---\n9\t\n10\t## 1. NOSS Identification\n11\t\n12\t| Field | Value |\n13\t|-------|-------|\n14\t| NOSS Code | G452-010-3:2023 |\n15\t| NOSS Title | Battery Electric Vehicle (BEV) Diagnostic and Rectification |\n16\t| NOSS Title (BM) | Diagnostik dan Pembaikan Kenderaan Elektrik Bateri (BEV) |\n17\t| Level | Three (3) |\n18\t| Section | (G) Wholesale and Retail Trade; Repair of Motor Vehicles and Motorcycles |\n19\t| Group | (452) Maintenance and Repair of Motor Vehicle |\n20\t| Area | Battery Electric Vehicle (BEV) Maintenance |\n21\t\n22\t## 2. Occupational Prerequisites\n23\t\n24\t- Minimum age 18 years old (Employment Act 1955 - Act 265)\n25\t- Medically fit (medical check-up by a licensed Medical Officer or Occupational Health Doctor)\n26\t- Pass colour blind test (verified by licensed Medical Officer)\n27\t- Certified BEV Technician (SKM Level 3) or equivalent\n28\t\n29\t## 3. Training Prerequisites (General)\n30\t\n31\tOne of the following certificates:\n32\t\n33\t1. G452-002-2:2018 Light Vehicle - Repair Service\n34\t2. G452-001-2:2017 Commercial Vehicles - Repair Service\n35\t3. TP-300-2:2013 Automotive After Sales Servicing\n36\t4. P-115-2 Mekanik Kenderaan Motor\n37\t5. P-119-2 Mekanik Kenderaan Motor\n38\t\n39\tPlus: Able to read and write (Bahasa Malaysia and/or English language)\n40\t\n41\t## 4. Regulatory/Statutory Requirements\n42\t\n43\t| Authority | Legislation |\n44\t|-----------|-------------|\n45\t| Department of Safety and Health | Occupational Safety and Health Act 1994 (Act 514) |\n46\t| Department of Environment | Environmental Quality Act 1974 (Act 127) |\n47\t| Department of Road Transport | Road Transport Act 1987 (Act 333) |\n48\t| Energy Commission (EC) | Energy Commission Act 2001; EC (Amendment) Act 2010; Electricity Supply Act 1990 (Act 447); Electricity Supply (Amendment) Act 2015 (Act A1501) |\n49\t\n50\t---\n51\t\n52\t## 5. Competency Profile Chart (CPC)\n53\t\n54\t### Summary of All Competency Units\n55\t\n56\t| CU Code | CU Title | Type | No. of WA |\n57\t|---------|----------|------|-----------|\n58\t| G452-010-3:2023-C01 | Perform BEV Scheduled Maintenance | Core | 3 |\n59\t| G452-010-3:2023-C02 | Perform Battery Management System (BMS) Rectification | Core | 6 |\n60\t| G452-010-3:2023-C03 | Perform BEV Thermal Cooling System Rectification | Core | 3 |\n61\t| G452-010-3:2023-C04 | Perform BEV Electric Drive Train System Rectification | Core | 5 |\n62\t| G452-010-3:2023-C05 | Perform BEV Workshop Supervisory Function | Core | 4 |\n63\t\n64\t### CPC Detail - Work Activities per CU\n65\t\n66\t#### CU C01: Perform BEV Scheduled Maintenance\n67\t\n68\t| WA Code | Work Activity |\n69\t|---------|---------------|\n70\t| G452-010-3:2023-C01-W01 | Carry Out BEV Schedule Maintenance Workplace Preparation |\n71\t| G452-010-3:2023-C01-W02 | Carry Out BEV Health Check Activities |\n72\t| G452-010-3:2023-C01-W03 | Carry Out BEV Maintenance Activities |\n73\t\n74\t#### CU C02: Perform Battery Management System (BMS) Rectification\n75\t\n76\t| WA Code | Work Activity |\n77\t|---------|---------------|\n78\t| G452-010-3:2023-C02-W01 | Carry Out Battery Management System (BMS) Rectification Workplace Preparation |\n79\t| G452-010-3:2023-C02-W02 | Carry Out BEV High Voltage Cable Replacement |\n80\t| G452-010-3:2023-C02-W03 | Carry Out BEV On-Board Charger (OBC) Replacement |\n81\t| G452-010-3:2023-C02-W04 | Carry Out BEV Pre-Charge Control System Servicing |\n82\t| G452-010-3:2023-C02-W05 | Carry Out BEV Traction Battery Pack Replacement |\n83\t| G452-010-3:2023-C02-W06 | Carry Out BEV DC-DC Converter Replacement |\n84\t\n85\t#### CU C03: Perform BEV Thermal Cooling System Rectification\n86\t\n87\t| WA Code | Work Activity |\n88\t|---------|---------------|\n89\t| G452-010-3:2023-C03-W01 | Carry Out Thermal Cooling System Rectification Workplace Preparation |\n90\t| G452-010-3:2023-C03-W02 | Carry Out BEV Liquid Cooling System Servicing |\n91\t| G452-010-3:2023-C03-W03 | Carry Out BEV Heating, Ventilation and Air Conditioning (HVAC) System Servicing |\n92\t\n93\t#### CU C04: Perform BEV Electric Drive Train System Rectification\n94\t\n95\t| WA Code | Work Activity |\n96\t|---------|---------------|\n97\t| G452-010-3:2023-C04-W01 | Carry Out Electric Drive Train System Rectification Workplace Preparation |\n98\t| G452-010-3:2023-C04-W02 | Carry Out BEV Inverter Unit Replacement |\n99\t| G452-010-3:2023-C04-W03 | Carry Out BEV Traction Motor Replacement |\n100\t| G452-010-3:2023-C04-W04 | Carry Out BEV Motorised Transaxle Servicing |"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4aad63e8-74bd-4073-a099-f779e2e73025","uuid":"6e38efb3-7961-4586-830d-2ef80536b16c","timestamp":"2026-04-16T14:50:14.353Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:/Users/Jyue/Documents/1-projects/noss-to-wim/bev-diagnostic-rectification/00-noss-extract.md","content":"# NOSS Extract: G452-010-3:2023 Battery Electric Vehicle (BEV) Diagnostic and Rectification\n\n> **Source:** G452-010-3:2023 Battery Electric Vehicle (BEV) Diagnostic and Rectification (138 pages)\n> **Level:** 3\n> **Extracted:** 2026-04-16\n> **Purpose:** Source-of-truth for WIM document generation\n\n---\n\n## 1. NOSS Identification\n\n| Field | Value |\n|-------|-------|\n| NOSS Code | G452-010-3:2023 |\n| NOSS Title | Battery Electric Vehicle (BEV) Diagnostic and Rectification |\n| NOSS Title (BM) | Diagnostik dan Pembaikan Kenderaan Elektrik Bateri (BEV) |\n| Level | Three (3) |\n| Section | (G) Wholesale and Retail Trade; Repair of Motor Vehicles and Motorcycles |\n| Group | (452) Maintenance and Repair of Motor Vehicle |\n| Area | Battery Electric Vehicle (BEV) Maintenance |\n\n## 2. Occupational Prerequisites\n\n- Minimum age 18 years old (Employment Act 1955 - Act 265)\n- Medically fit (medical check-up by a licensed Medical Officer or Occupational Health Doctor)\n- Pass colour blind test (verified by licensed Medical Officer)\n- Certified BEV Technician (SKM Level 3) or equivalent\n\n## 3. Training Prerequisites (General)\n\nOne of the following certificates:\n\n1. G452-002-2:2018 Light Vehicle - Repair Service\n2. G452-001-2:2017 Commercial Vehicles - Repair Service\n3. TP-300-2:2013 Automotive After Sales Servicing\n4. P-115-2 Mekanik Kenderaan Motor\n5. P-119-2 Mekanik Kenderaan Motor\n\nPlus: Able to read and write (Bahasa Malaysia and/or English language)\n\n## 4. Regulatory/Statutory Requirements\n\n| Authority | Legislation |\n|-----------|-------------|\n| Department of Safety and Health | Occupational Safety and Health Act 1994 (Act 514) |\n| Department of Environment | Environmental Quality Act 1974 (Act 127) |\n| Department of Road Transport | Road Transport Act 1987 (Act 333) |\n| Energy Commission (EC) | Energy Commission Act 2001; EC (Amendment) Act 2010; Electricity Supply Act 1990 (Act 447); Electricity Supply (Amendment) Act 2015 (Act A1501) |\n\n---\n\n## 5. Competency Profile Chart (CPC)\n\n### Summary of All Competency Units\n\n| CU Code | CU Title | Type | No. of WA |\n|---------|----------|------|-----------|\n| G452-010-3:2023-C01 | Perform BEV Scheduled Maintenance | Core | 3 |\n| G452-010-3:2023-C02 | Perform Battery Management System (BMS) Rectification | Core | 6 |\n| G452-010-3:2023-C03 | Perform BEV Thermal Cooling System Rectification | Core | 3 |\n| G452-010-3:2023-C04 | Perform BEV Electric Drive Train System Rectification | Core | 5 |\n| G452-010-3:2023-C05 | Perform BEV Workshop Supervisory Function | Core | 4 |\n\n### CPC Detail - Work Activities per CU\n\n#### CU C01: Perform BEV Scheduled Maintenance\n\n| WA Code | Work Activity |\n|---------|---------------|\n| G452-010-3:2023-C01-W01 | Carry Out BEV Schedule Maintenance Workplace Preparation |\n| G452-010-3:2023-C01-W02 | Carry Out BEV Health Check Activities |\n| G452-010-3:2023-C01-W03 | Carry Out BEV Maintenance Activities |\n\n#### CU C02: Perform Battery Management System (BMS) Rectification\n\n| WA Code | Work Activity |\n|---------|---------------|\n| G452-010-3:2023-C02-W01 | Carry Out Battery Management System (BMS) Rectification Workplace Preparation |\n| G452-010-3:2023-C02-W02 | Carry Out BEV High Voltage Cable Replacement |\n| G452-010-3:2023-C02-W03 | Carry Out BEV On-Board Charger (OBC) Replacement |\n| G452-010-3:2023-C02-W04 | Carry Out BEV Pre-Charge Control System Servicing |\n| G452-010-3:2023-C02-W05 | Carry Out BEV Traction Battery Pack Replacement |\n| G452-010-3:2023-C02-W06 | Carry Out BEV DC-DC Converter Replacement |\n\n#### CU C03: Perform BEV Thermal Cooling System Rectification\n\n| WA Code | Work Activity |\n|---------|---------------|\n| G452-010-3:2023-C03-W01 | Carry Out Thermal Cooling System Rectification Workplace Preparation |\n| G452-010-3:2023-C03-W02 | Carry Out BEV Liquid Cooling System Servicing |\n| G452-010-3:2023-C03-W03 | Carry Out BEV Heating, Ventilation and Air Conditioning (HVAC) System Servicing |\n\n#### CU C04: Perform BEV Electric Drive Train System Rectification\n\n| WA Code | Work Activity |\n|---------|---------------|\n| G452-010-3:2023-C04-W01 | Carry Out Electric Drive Train System Rectification Workplace Preparation |\n| G452-010-3:2023-C04-W02 | Carry Out BEV Inverter Unit Replacement |\n| G452-010-3:2023-C04-W03 | Carry Out BEV Traction Motor Replacement |\n| G452-010-3:2023-C04-W04 | Carry Out BEV Motorised Transaxle Servicing |","numLines":100,"startLine":1,"totalLines":962}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_012YcRvs2etrekrFAAgYZZiE","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01SB9e6YArk6pzizwvaSg99L","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"C:/Users/Jyue/Documents/1-projects/noss-to-wim/bev-diagnostic-rectification/C02/KP-01.md","limit":60},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":9102,"cache_read_input_tokens":36266,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":9102},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4aad63e8-74bd-4073-a099-f779e2e73025","uuid":"e1143fca-b762-4f90-b5e1-860a5f5949e3"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SB9e6YArk6pzizwvaSg99L","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# KERTAS PENERANGAN / INFORMATION SHEET\n2\t\n3\t**Kod WIM / WIM Code:** G452-010-3:2023-C02/KP(1/6)\n4\t**Muka / Page:** 1\n5\t**Kertas Warna / Paper Color:** PUTIH / WHITE\n6\t\n7\t---\n8\t\n9\t## Competency Unit: Perform Battery Management System (BMS) Rectification\n10\t## Work Activity 1: Carry Out BMS Rectification Workplace Preparation\n11\t\n12\t---\n13\t\n14\t## Objektif Pembelajaran / Learning Objectives\n15\t\n16\tUpon completion of this Information Sheet, trainees will be able to:\n17\t1. State the licensing, legislative, and regulatory requirements for BMS rectification work\n18\t2. Explain BMS rectification safety and health requirements, including HV-specific PPE\n19\t3. List HV-rated tools and equipment required for BMS rectification\n20\t4. Describe hazard identification specific to BMS rectification work\n21\t5. Explain scheduled waste management procedures for BMS-related waste\n22\t\n23\t---\n24\t\n25\t## 0. BMS Component Overview (Gambaran Keseluruhan Komponen BMS)\n26\t\n27\tThe Battery Management System (BMS) — *Sistem Pengurusan Bateri* — is an electronic control unit that monitors, protects, and manages the HV traction battery. BMS rectification work covers the following components:\n28\t\n29\t| Component | BM Term | Function | Failure Mode |\n30\t|-----------|---------|----------|--------------|\n31\t| BMS master controller | Pengawal Induk BMS | Monitors overall pack, communicates with VCU | Communication fault, software error |\n32\t| Main positive contactor | Penyentuh Positif Utama | Connects battery positive to HV bus | Weld failure (stuck closed), open circuit |\n33\t| Main negative contactor | Penyentuh Negatif Utama | Connects battery negative to HV bus | Weld failure, coil open circuit |\n34\t| Pre-charge relay | Geganti Pra-cas | Limits inrush current on start-up | Open circuit, resistor degradation |\n35\t| Current sensor | Penderia Arus | Measures charge/discharge current | Drift, offset error, signal failure |\n36\t| HV cables | Kabel Voltan Tinggi | Transmit HV power between components | Insulation breakdown, connector corrosion |\n37\t| Service plug / interlock | Plag Servis / Interlock | Manual HV isolation point | Broken locking mechanism, connector damage |\n38\t\n39\t---\n40\t\n41\t## 1. BEV Rules and Regulations for BMS Rectification\n42\t\n43\tBMS rectification work involves direct interaction with HV components operating at 200-800V DC. This carries significantly higher risk than scheduled maintenance (C01). All regulatory requirements from C01/KP(1/3) apply, plus additional requirements:\n44\t\n45\t### 1.1 Additional Certification Requirements\n46\t- Technicians performing BMS rectification must hold specific HV safety certification\n47\t- The Energy Commission may require additional electrical worker licensing for HV battery work\n48\t- Workshop must have documented HV work procedures reviewed and approved by a competent person\n49\t\n50\t### 1.2 Regulatory Compliance for HV Work\n51\t\n52\t| Requirement | Standard | Details |\n53\t|-------------|----------|---------|\n54\t| HV work permit | Company SOP | Written permit required before commencing any HV disconnection |\n55\t| Two-person rule | OSHA 1994 best practice | Minimum two qualified persons present during HV work |\n56\t| Emergency preparedness | OSHA 1994 | CPR/AED-trained personnel must be on-site |\n57\t| HV area demarcation | IEC 61010 | Physical barriers with HV warning signage |\n58\t\n59\t---\n60\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4aad63e8-74bd-4073-a099-f779e2e73025","uuid":"3e2fa086-1f48-4ad8-bad7-088612790cc0","timestamp":"2026-04-16T14:50:15.451Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:/Users/Jyue/Documents/1-projects/noss-to-wim/bev-diagnostic-rectification/C02/KP-01.md","content":"# KERTAS PENERANGAN / INFORMATION SHEET\n\n**Kod WIM / WIM Code:** G452-010-3:2023-C02/KP(1/6)\n**Muka / Page:** 1\n**Kertas Warna / Paper Color:** PUTIH / WHITE\n\n---\n\n## Competency Unit: Perform Battery Management System (BMS) Rectification\n## Work Activity 1: Carry Out BMS Rectification Workplace Preparation\n\n---\n\n## Objektif Pembelajaran / Learning Objectives\n\nUpon completion of this Information Sheet, trainees will be able to:\n1. State the licensing, legislative, and regulatory requirements for BMS rectification work\n2. Explain BMS rectification safety and health requirements, including HV-specific PPE\n3. List HV-rated tools and equipment required for BMS rectification\n4. Describe hazard identification specific to BMS rectification work\n5. Explain scheduled waste management procedures for BMS-related waste\n\n---\n\n## 0. BMS Component Overview (Gambaran Keseluruhan Komponen BMS)\n\nThe Battery Management System (BMS) — *Sistem Pengurusan Bateri* — is an electronic control unit that monitors, protects, and manages the HV traction battery. BMS rectification work covers the following components:\n\n| Component | BM Term | Function | Failure Mode |\n|-----------|---------|----------|--------------|\n| BMS master controller | Pengawal Induk BMS | Monitors overall pack, communicates with VCU | Communication fault, software error |\n| Main positive contactor | Penyentuh Positif Utama | Connects battery positive to HV bus | Weld failure (stuck closed), open circuit |\n| Main negative contactor | Penyentuh Negatif Utama | Connects battery negative to HV bus | Weld failure, coil open circuit |\n| Pre-charge relay | Geganti Pra-cas | Limits inrush current on start-up | Open circuit, resistor degradation |\n| Current sensor | Penderia Arus | Measures charge/discharge current | Drift, offset error, signal failure |\n| HV cables | Kabel Voltan Tinggi | Transmit HV power between components | Insulation breakdown, connector corrosion |\n| Service plug / interlock | Plag Servis / Interlock | Manual HV isolation point | Broken locking mechanism, connector damage |\n\n---\n\n## 1. BEV Rules and Regulations for BMS Rectification\n\nBMS rectification work involves direct interaction with HV components operating at 200-800V DC. This carries significantly higher risk than scheduled maintenance (C01). All regulatory requirements from C01/KP(1/3) apply, plus additional requirements:\n\n### 1.1 Additional Certification Requirements\n- Technicians performing BMS rectification must hold specific HV safety certification\n- The Energy Commission may require additional electrical worker licensing for HV battery work\n- Workshop must have documented HV work procedures reviewed and approved by a competent person\n\n### 1.2 Regulatory Compliance for HV Work\n\n| Requirement | Standard | Details |\n|-------------|----------|---------|\n| HV work permit | Company SOP | Written permit required before commencing any HV disconnection |\n| Two-person rule | OSHA 1994 best practice | Minimum two qualified persons present during HV work |\n| Emergency preparedness | OSHA 1994 | CPR/AED-trained personnel must be on-site |\n| HV area demarcation | IEC 61010 | Physical barriers with HV warning signage |\n\n---\n","numLines":60,"startLine":1,"totalLines":190}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01A5R2ui9nHT7gGpdcZwr9w9","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have a good understanding. Let me look at the C02 KP files briefly to understand the topics for the 40+ questions needed.\n\nC02 has 6 KP files covering:\n1. KP-01: BMS Workplace Preparation (safety, regulations, PPE, tools)\n2. KP-02: HV Cable Replacement (likely HV cable systems)\n3. KP-03: OBC (On-Board Charger)\n4. KP-04: Pre-Charge Control System\n5. KP-05: Traction Battery Pack Replacement\n6. KP-06: DC-DC Converter Replacement\n\n\nThe acceptance criteria specifies a 35% BMS theory, 35% HV cable systems, and 30% OBC distribution, which breaks down to roughly 14 questions on BMS components and theory, 14 on HV cable systems, and 12 on OBC operation. Now I need to plan out how to structure these questions across the knowledge points.\n\nFor the implementation, I'm organizing the C02 question bank into sections: multiple choice questions covering BMS theory first, then moving into HV cable systems, followed by graded scenario questions that test deeper understanding with longer answers. I'm working through the distribution more carefully now. With 40 questions total, I need BMS theory and HV cable systems at 35% each (14 questions each) and OBC at 30% (12 questions). For the scenario requirement of 30%, that's 12 scenario questions out of 40. So I'm structuring it as 20 MCQ questions in Section A worth 20 marks, then 5 scenario questions in Section B worth 5 marks, with 15 enrichment questions in Section C carrying no marks—this gives me 20 total scenarios across sections B and C, which exceeds the 30% threshold. Now I'm mapping out the specific breakdown: Section A has 7 BMS theory MCQs, 7 HV cable system MCQs, and 6 OBC MCQs. I'm realizing the topic distribution won't align perfectly with the question type sections, so I need to organize this differently. I'll structure it as three sections—multiple choice questions covering BMS, HV cables, and OBC; then graded scenarios mixing all topics; and finally enrichment questions for deeper practice—while noting the overall topic percentages at the top of the question bank. The scenario questions in Section B have detailed sample answers of 50+ words each, and Section C contains 15 enrichment questions worth zero marks that dive deeper into all three topics. Looking at the distribution, I'm getting roughly equal coverage across BMS, HV cable, and OBC systems, though it doesn't perfectly match the original 35/35/30 split I was aiming for.\n\nLet me restructure this using a simpler range-based approach like the reference pattern—assigning BMS theory to Q1–Q14, HV Cable Systems to Q15–Q28, and OBC to Q29–Q40, which gives me 14, 14, and 12 questions respectively for a 35/35/30 distribution. The question types will then layer across these ranges, with MCQs filling Q1–Q20 and scenario-based questions distributed throughout the remaining sections. for Q21–Q25 (5 questions in the BMS/HV Cable range)\n- Enrichment for Q26–Q40 (in HV Cable and OBC ranges)\n\nThis means:\nSection A MCQ: Q1–Q20 (20 questions)\n- Q1–Q14: BMS theory MCQ (14)\n- Q15–Q20: Start of HV Cable MCQ (6)\n\nSection B Graded Scenarios: Q21–Q25 (5 questions)\n- Q21–Q25: Mix of topics (mostly completing HV Cable Q21–Q25 is in that range Q15–Q28)\n\nSection C Enrichment: Q26–Q40 (15 questions)\n- Q26–Q28: End of HV Cable (3 enrichment)\n- Q29–Q40: OBC (12 enrichment)\n\nNow I'm checking the distribution: BMS covers Q1–Q14 as MCQ, HV Cable spans Q15–Q28 with 6 MCQ plus 5 graded scenarios plus 3 enrichment questions, and OBC is Q29–Q40 with 12 enrichment questions. The graded scenarios total 5 questions while enrichment adds 15 more, giving 20 out of 40 total as scenario-based, which meets the 30% minimum. However, I realize the enrichment questions in C26–Q40 also need sample answers to satisfy the requirement that all scenario-based questions across both files have 50+ word answers. with sample answers, and C01 needs answers for Q26–Q35 while C02 needs a full Q-format section with MCQs plus graded and enrichment scenarios. This gets both courses to at least 42.9% coverage, meeting the 30% threshold. Now I'm checking the diff size constraints—adding sample answers for the 10 enrichment questions in C01 should fit comfortably within the ~350 line limit.\n\nFor C02, I'm looking at the scope more carefully. The acceptance criteria require 40+ questions with a 25-mark answer key and CoCU references, which means I need to be strategic about how I structure the Q-format section to stay within the budget while hitting all the requirements.\n\nFor C01, I'm realizing the current file already has the 35 questions and answer key in place, but I need to ensure at least 30% are scenario-based with substantial answers. That means I need about 11 scenario questions total—currently there are 5 with answers, so I should add sample answers to 6 more enrichment questions to meet this threshold without exceeding the line limit.\n\nFor C02, the existing file only has around 18 questions across sections A-C, so I need to build out a full Q-Format section to reach 40 questions. I'm planning a structure with 20 MCQ questions, 12 scenario-based questions with 50+ word answers to hit the 30% requirement, and 8 enrichment questions. This layout should fit within reasonable space constraints while meeting all the requirements.\n\nFor C01, I just need to add sample answers to six of the existing scenario questions to demonstrate the expected depth. The overall approach is to append a complete Q-Format bank section to C02 rather than modifying the existing content, keeping the diff minimal and preserving what's already there.\n\nNow I'm mapping out the question distribution for C02's new Q-Format section—roughly a third covering BMS fundamentals like pre-charge circuits and cell balancing, another third on HV cable systems including color coding and insulation specs, and the final third on onboard charger functionality and charging standards. Now I'm laying out the specific BMS theory questions covering pre-charge relays, cell balancing methods, SOC calculation, insulation standards, CMU monitoring, fault diagnostics, module failure thresholds, service procedures, de-energization timing, CAN communication, and thermal runaway conditions.\n\nMoving into HV cable specifications—orange identification, insulation resistance requirements, connector torque for gas-tight seals, megger testing protocols, crimping equipment, and routing safety constraints.\n\nThe exam structure shifts to graded scenarios worth five marks with mixed BMS, HV, and OBC situations requiring detailed written responses, followed by enrichment scenarios.\n\nI'm realizing the scenario distribution needs adjustment—I need twelve scenarios total to hit 30% of the forty-question exam, which means adding seven more graded scenarios with sample answers beyond the initial five.\n\nLet me reorganize by topic instead: BMS gets 35% (fourteen questions), HV Cable gets 35% (fourteen questions), and OBC gets 30% (twelve questions). The first twenty questions are all multiple choice for twenty marks, then I'll layer in the scenario questions and enrichment material across the remaining slots. Now I'm working through the BMS theory questions, covering current measurement components, de-energization timing requirements, contactor failure modes, and cell balancing mechanisms. Continuing through the battery management questions—cell voltage deviation needs to stay within ±0.05V, thermal runaway becomes a serious concern once cells hit 80°C, and the interlock plug provides that critical manual isolation point for high-voltage safety. The next question is asking about minimum personnel requirements, which I'm still reading through. Orange sheath is the correct identifier for HV cables in BEVs. Moving through the cable specifications now—insulation resistance needs to be at least 1 MΩ for in-service cables, and post-replacement testing requires a 500V DC megger test. For terminal crimping, I need to note that a hydraulic crimping tool with the correct die is essential, not improvised methods.\n\nHV cable routing is critical—they must be kept separate from low-voltage harnesses and protected from sharp edges, heat sources, and moving parts. Connector torque specifications are equally important because insufficient torque creates resistance heating while over-torquing damages the seal, both causing failures.\n\nNow looking at the graded scenario questions that require detailed 50+ word responses. Looking at Q23, I need to work through the AC input voltage fault systematically—starting with verifying the charging station output, then checking the vehicle's AC input circuitry, connectors, and onboard charger diagnostics to isolate whether the issue is external or internal. For Q24, the 42 Ω pre-charge resistor is below spec and unacceptable; installing it would allow excessive inrush current during contactors closure, risking component damage and nuisance faults. Q25 addresses why P0A80 persists after battery replacement—the code typically requires a specific BMS initialization procedure or module reprogramming rather than just clearing, and I'd need to follow the manufacturer's protocol to properly register the new battery pack before the fault clears.\n\nNow moving into the next set of questions covering HV de-energisation procedures, cell balancing strategies, HV cable replacement workflows, and onboard charger specifications. Looking at the enrichment practice questions covering battery pack replacement procedures, HV cable verification, charging protocols specific to Malaysia's infrastructure, intermittent insulation faults and their diagnostic approaches, CCS communication protocols for DC fast charging, and what appears to be another question starting to form.\n\nNow I'm seeing the continuation with battery conditioning procedures before long journeys and their effects on pack lifespan, the five functional checks needed after onboard charger replacement, and how the battery management system's thermal algorithm protects cells during rapid charging with its two primary protective responses.\n\nI'm also noting the mark distribution for the assessment—the main section carries 25 marks total across 25 questions, while these enrichment questions don't contribute to the final score.\n\nLooking at the scenario coverage across both documents, I need to ensure I'm hitting that 30% threshold. The current setup has some graded scenarios, but combining both files I'm falling short. I'm calculating that I need to expand the scenario questions significantly—adding more to C01 and ensuring C02 has adequate coverage so that together they represent at least 30% of the total question bank.\n\nNow I'm checking the diff size impact. Adding sample answers to those extra questions in C01 will add roughly 80 lines, and the new Q-Format section in C02 brings another 200 lines, so I'm looking at around 280 lines total, which should fit within the budget.\n\nBut I'm noticing something—some of the questions I'm planning to add answers for in C01 don't actually align with the \"Battery Basics\" section they're labeled under. Q26 is about PPE donning, Q27 covers hazard versus risk, Q29 deals with flood-damaged vehicles, Q30 is about zero-energy verification, and Q31 focuses on the Hierarchy of Controls—these are all safety-related topics, not battery basics. I need to reconsider which questions actually belong in this section and whether I'm adding answers to the right ones.\n\nLet me be more precise about the line count. If each sample answer needs at least 50 words and I'm working with roughly 7-10 words per line, that's about 5-7 lines per answer. Adding the question itself, a blank line, and some spacing, I'm looking at 7-9 lines per question. For 10 questions, that's around 80 lines for C01, which combined with the 200 lines for the new Q-Format section in C02 keeps me under the overall diff limit. I'm recalculating the line counts more precisely. Each MCQ fits on a single line with the question, all four options, and the CoCU code. The graded scenarios take about 4-5 lines each when including the sample answer. Let me work through the C02 section breakdown again: the header takes a few lines, then 20 MCQs are just 20 lines total, followed by the graded questions with their sample answers at roughly 5 lines per question.\n\nFor C01, I need to add sample answers to Q21-Q25, which means about 3-4 lines per question for the answer text. The total additions should come to around 150 lines, which is well within the safe range.\n\nThe key challenge now is writing those sample answers to be substantive—at least 50 words each—while keeping them concise enough to fit the format. the main positive contactor closes without the pre-charge resistor limiting inrush current, causing a massive voltage spike across the capacitor bank that can damage the battery management system and other electronics — this is why pre-charge circuit integrity is critical before any HV work begins.\n\nQ29 - Flood-damaged BEV: Submerged lithium cells present hidden hazards like internal short circuits that may trigger thermal runaway hours or days after the flood, and moisture trapped inside the battery housing creates unpredictable high-voltage leakage paths.  The proper response is to isolate the vehicle at least 10 meters away, maintain a 48-hour fire watch, avoid any attempt to power on or inspect internally, contact the manufacturer's emergency line, and document everything with photos before the workshop supervisor takes action.\n\nQ30 - Zero-energy verification: I'm working through the systematic testing procedure using a calibrated CAT III multimeter — first confirming the meter itself works on a known live circuit, then after waiting 5-10 minutes post-de-energization, testing the main positive terminal at the HV junction box to confirm zero voltage.\n\nTesting the remaining critical points...\n\nThe hierarchy of controls framework ranks interventions from most to least effective: elimination removes the hazard entirely, substitution replaces it with something safer, engineering controls physically isolate the risk, administrative controls modify procedures, and PPE serves as the final layer of protection. For high-voltage work on battery electric vehicles, this means preferring to decommission damaged packs rather than repair them, using safer insulating materials, installing interlocks that cut power when accessed, requiring two-person authorization before HV tasks, and equipping technicians with insulated gloves and arc-rated protective gear.\n\nBattery pack replacement becomes necessary when state of health drops below 80%, at which point the battery management system begins restricting performance to protect degraded cells—reducing charge and discharge rates, limiting available energy to the weakest cell's capacity, and cutting regenerative braking current to prevent thermal runaway, though these protections progressively reduce vehicle range and performance.\n\nBefore selecting PPE for high-voltage work, I need to identify the system voltage by checking the vehicle's technical documentation and manufacturer labels, then confirming with a non-contact voltage tester if needed, since Class 0 gloves suit standard 400V systems while 800V systems typically require Class 1 protection.\n\nFor range reduction issues, I'm considering three battery degradation mechanisms: cell capacity loss measured through BMS health data, cell imbalance where weaker cells limit overall pack discharge, and electrolyte decomposition that increases internal resistance and voltage sag under load.\n\nOn the regulatory side, I'm noting that glycol coolant falls under Scheduled Waste SW305 and requires licensed contractor disposal with three-year manifold record retention, while lead-acid batteries as SW102 must go to licensed recyclers with strict handling requirements—violations carry penalties up to RM500,000 under Malaysian environmental law.\n\nNow I'm structuring a 40-question exam covering BMS theory, high-voltage cable systems, and onboard chargers, distributed across multiple choice and short answer sections with a total of 25 marks. Now I'm working through questions on cell balancing, battery health thresholds, voltage tolerances, and thermal safety limits for high-voltage systems. Continuing through the pre-charge circuit questions — a timeout fault means the capacitors didn't reach their threshold voltage in time, likely due to resistor degradation or an open circuit. Pre-charge resistors typically fall in the 50–200 Ω range, and HV cables are identified by their distinctive orange sheath or conduit. I'm also checking the minimum insulation resistance requirements for in-service HV cables. The HV cable routing requirements specify avoiding sharp edges, heat sources, and moving parts while keeping separate from low-voltage wiring. Under-torqued HV connectors create resistance heating at contact points that can lead to thermal failure. Now I'm looking at a scenario question about battery management system diagnostics where Module 4 cells are showing voltage deviations below the pack average. replacement is required and I need to escalate this to a C02 work order, ensuring full HV de-energisation procedures are completed before removing the battery pack.\n\nNow I'm moving to the next diagnostic scenario involving insulation testing on a replaced HV cable. The reading of 750 kΩ falls short of the 1 MΩ minimum specification, which indicates a partial insulation failure—likely from cable pinching during routing, an improperly seated connector seal, or contamination at the crimp point.\n\nReturning the vehicle with this insulation level is risky because it could lead to progressive breakdown, triggering IMD faults and creating electrocution hazards. I need to systematically trace and inspect the entire cable run, verify all connector seals and crimp joints, re-route if there's any pinching, and then re-test after each correction until the reading exceeds 1 MΩ.\n\nMoving on to the next question about a BEV that won't complete AC charging with fault code P0C66 indicating an out-of-range input voltage. Since the charging station itself is confirmed working, this points to an issue on the vehicle's OBC AC input side rather than the external charger.\n\nMy diagnostic approach would be to first inspect the Type 2 charging port for bent pins, moisture, or corrosion and clean as needed, then check the AC harness from the port to the OBC for damaged insulation, corroded connectors, and loose terminals. With the HV system de-energized, I'd measure resistance continuity across each AC phase wire to detect any open circuits in the harness. Next, I'd measure the AC inlet voltage using a verified working station to confirm 230V is actually reaching the OBC input terminal. If voltage is present but the fault persists, the OBC's internal AC-DC conversion stage has failed and requires replacement following the proper procedure.\n\nNow looking at the pre-charge scenario — an extended 3.8-second duration against the 0.5–2.0 second specification indicates excessive pre-charge resistance in the circuit. This could stem from an incorrect resistor value being installed, corrosion or high-resistance joints in the pre-charge relay harness adding series resistance, or a partially open main positive contactor restricting the current path. While not immediately destructive, exceeding 5–10 seconds risks triggering a pre-charge timeout fault code, so I need to verify the replacement resistor matches the manufacturer specification and measure the circuit to identify where the excess resistance is coming from.\n\nMoving to the next scenario about the persistent P0A80 fault after battery pack replacement — the BMS likely requires a relearn or reset procedure to accept the new pack's factory calibration data, since the vehicle control unit still holds the previous pack's state-of-health history and won't automatically clear the fault code until the new pack's baseline parameters are registered in the system. MS module is paired to the vehicle using manufacturer diagnostic software (variant coding/adaptation); (2) the new pack's SOC is brought to a known reference state (typically 50% charge) before pairing; (3) a BMS reset sequence is performed via the OEM diagnostic tool to clear the stored degradation parameters and accept the new pack's cell chemistry baseline. Without this procedure, the VCU continues to reference the old pack's parameters, causing persistent fault codes. Consult the manufacturer's Technical Service Bulletin for the specific relearn procedure.\n\n### Bahagian C / Section C — Senario Pengayaan / Enrichment Practice Scenarios\n\n*Soalan latihan untuk kefahaman mendalam. / Practice questions for deeper understanding.*\n\n**Q26.** Describe the complete seven-step HV de-energisation procedure for BMS rectification work in correct sequence. Explain the purpose of each step.\n\nNow I'm working through the seven-step de-energisation sequence: turning off the READY switch to open the main contactors, securing the ignition key to prevent accidental activation, verifying the correct vehicle identity, disconnecting the 12V auxiliary battery to cut power to the BMS and control circuits, removing the service plug for a physical mid-pack voltage break, waiting several minutes for capacitor discharge, and finally verifying zero energy state before beginning work. –95% transfer efficiency compared to passive balancing's zero energy recovery. Active balancing also keeps cells in a more optimal state with less thermal stress, which slows degradation and extends the pack's useful life before it hits the 80% state-of-health threshold. Now moving to the HV cable replacement procedure, which requires full de-energisation, documenting the existing routing and connector specifications, then carefully disconnecting the orange HV connectors.\n\nContinuing through the replacement steps: removing the cable clips and conduit, extracting the old cable while noting its bend radius and path, verifying the new cable matches the original specifications, routing it identically with proper bend radius and securing it with new clips, reconnecting both terminals to torque specification, restoring power, and finally running a megger insulation test at 500V DC to confirm at least 1 MΩ resistance before re-energising the system.\n\nThe OBC's Power Factor Correction stage aligns the AC input current waveform with the voltage waveform from the grid. Without it, the charger would draw current in sharp pulses rather than smoothly, creating harmonics that drag the power factor down to around 0.5–0.6 instead of the ideal 1.0, forcing the grid to supply far more apparent power than the OBC actually consumes and stressing distribution transformers. For Malaysia's grid, this matters increasingly as more BEVs charge simultaneously — poor power factor across thousands of vehicles would degrade voltage stability, so modern OBCs maintain power factor above 0.99 to act as nearly ideal loads on the supply.\n\nFor the coolant drain procedure, I need to walk through the safe steps: let the OBC cool completely, position a labelled collection container beneath the outlet, use insulated tools to loosen the lower hose clamp and let gravity drain the coolant, remove the inlet hose to vent and fully empty the circuit, cap both hoses immediately, then transfer the drained coolant to a sealed container labelled as Scheduled Waste SW305 (glycol coolant from a BEV HV system), which falls under Malaysia's Environmental Quality Scheduled Wastes Regulations 2005.\n\nNow I'm moving into the DC-DC converter diagnostics question. I need to identify five key parameters to test when suspecting a faulty converter and explain what abnormal readings mean. The first parameter is the 12V output voltage, which should sit between 13.5–14.4V — if it drops below 13V while the HV system is active, that points to either a failed output stage or the converter not engaging properly. The second is measuring 12V system current draw using a clamp meter at the 12V bus; excessive current suggests a short circuit in the 12V load side overwhelming the converter. The third involves checking the HV input voltage feeding the converter, which should match the main HV bus voltage around 200V.\n\nFor the fourth parameter, I'm looking at converter casing temperature using a thermal camera — anything beyond 70°C indicates internal faults or cooling system problems. The fifth is reading diagnostic fault codes, where U-codes point to CAN communication failures between the DC-DC ECU and VCU, while P-codes reveal voltage regulation issues. U-codes take priority since communication loss prevents proper system diagnosis.\n\nNow I'm addressing the OBC charging initiation failure scenario. The vehicle shows READY status with no fault codes, yet charging won't start, so I need to rank the five most likely causes by diagnostic priority. The first suspect is the charging port proximity sensor or lock actuator. ...parameters, OBC will not commence charging; I need to capture CAN bus data and verify the BMS-to-OBC message stream is actually present.\n\nNow I'm looking at some practice questions covering battery module replacement strategies, HV cable phase identification procedures, Malaysia's charging standards with their connector and power specifications, and a scenario about recurring insulation faults.\n\nThe scenario involves a BEV with intermittent HV insulation codes that clear after overnight storage in a dry environment—I should identify three probable causes and outline the diagnostic approach. Then there are questions on V2G protocol relevance to Malaysia's energy plans, battery conditioning in different climates, the five functional verification tests after OBC replacement, and how the BMS thermal protection algorithm throttles charging when cell temperatures approach limits.\n\nNow I'm looking at the answer key for the multiple-choice section, which shows the correct answers for questions 1 through 20, each worth one mark, followed by the structure for section B covering questions 21 through 25.\n\nFor section B, I need to award one mark when a response correctly identifies the key technical finding and references the relevant CoCU assessment criteria. Section C is an enrichment section with no marks, where guided sample answers are provided for questions 26 through 32, and the trainer will provide sample answers for the remaining questions to facilitate discussion.\n\nThe total marks for this Q-Format assessment is 25, with a passing threshold of 60 percent or 15 marks, and all assessment criteria are based on the CoCU C02 reference standard.\n\nI'm now checking the topic distribution across the C02 Q-Format to see how the questions break down by content area. I'm realizing the issue—the questions Q21-Q25 are labeled as scenarios but they're scattered across different topics. Q21 is actually BMS theory, Q22 is HV cable, Q23 is OBC, Q24 relates to pre-charge which could be BMS, and Q25 is also BMS. So these scenario questions don't align with the stated range distribution where Q15-Q28 should all be HV Cable topics.\n\nLooking back at the file structure, the distribution header seems to be more of a rough guideline for overall percentages rather than a strict per-question mapping. The actual content mixes topics throughout, so I'll keep my Q-Format as planned—the header indicates the approximate breakdown by count, but the individual questions will naturally span across different topics within those ranges. I'm realizing the issue—Q21-Q25 aren't strictly bound to the HV Cable topic block even though they fall within that question range. Looking at how C01 structures this, the scenario questions are labeled as their own section and can cover mixed topics, so the distribution header is just showing the overall Q-Format breakdown rather than enforcing topic alignment for every individual question. I'll keep my current structure since the scenario questions naturally span multiple diagnostic areas. I see the issue—I'm overthinking the topic distribution. The validation criteria only care about CoCU references and question count, not perfect alignment with the headers. Let me refocus on content quality and consistency instead. For Q26-Q28 in the HV Cable range, I'll shift them to genuinely cover HV cable topics: cable identification and routing, connector torque and sealing specifications, and then move into the next section. I'm settling on a structure that balances content distribution across the question range while meeting all the acceptance criteria—20 MCQs covering BMS and HV cable theory, followed by 12 graded and enrichment questions with sample answers that make up the required 30% scenario component, then 8 additional practice questions for guided discussion.\n\nNow I need to handle the C01 modifications similarly. The current file has Q26-Q35 as enrichment questions without sample answers, so I'll need to add those to reach the 15 total scenarios with answers across C01—combining the 5 graded scenarios from Q21-Q25 with 10 new sample answers for Q26-Q35, which gives me the 42.9% coverage needed. Let me examine how Q26-Q35 are currently formatted so I can insert the sample answers cleanly without altering the question text itself. I'm working through the formatting for these exam questions. I need to add sample answers to Q26-Q35, inserting a blank line after each question followed by the answer template in both Malay and English, then another blank line before the next question. This will expand the section significantly as each answer needs roughly 50-80 words of content.\n\nFor the C02 section, I'm building the entire Q-Format from scratch, which includes multiple choice questions in sections A and B, then structured questions in section C with full sample answers. I'm estimating the total additions across both components and planning how to organize the content efficiently while maintaining consistent formatting throughout. I've calculated the line estimates and confirmed everything fits within the budget at around 252 lines total. Now I'm ready to start writing the actual content, making sure to match the established format carefully. Before I begin, I need to check what CoCU codes are available for the C02 assessment to reference them correctly in the document.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6794,"cache_read_input_tokens":45368,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":6794},"output_tokens":13,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4aad63e8-74bd-4073-a099-f779e2e73025","uuid":"fc78b481-d1b2-443d-850b-ea1cffee8162"}
