{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01A8LeNbVpuue2er5gbSrV7d","type":"tool_result","content":"100\t| G452-010-3:2023-C04-W04 | Carry Out BEV Motorised Transaxle Servicing |\n101\t| G452-010-3:2023-C04-W05 | Carry Out BEV Integrated Brake Control System Replacement |\n102\t\n103\t#### CU C05: Perform BEV Workshop Supervisory Function\n104\t\n105\t| WA Code | Work Activity |\n106\t|---------|---------------|\n107\t| G452-010-3:2023-C05-W01 | Conduct BEV Daily Briefing |\n108\t| G452-010-3:2023-C05-W02 | Monitor BEV Subordinate's Performance |\n109\t| G452-010-3:2023-C05-W03 | Coordinate BEV Subordinate's Training |\n110\t| G452-010-3:2023-C05-W04 | Monitor BEV Workshop Safety, Health & Environment |\n111\t\n112\t---\n113\t\n114\t## 6. Competency Profile (CP)\n115\t\n116\t### 6.1 CU C01: Perform BEV Scheduled Maintenance (G452-010-3:2023-C01)\n117\t\n118\t**CU Descriptor:** Perform BEV Scheduled Maintenance preliminary is the first and necessary steps to set-up a working place. This is to determine the working environment for servicing and maintenance of BEV are equipped, safe and suitable. Check the function and condition of safety equipment and component of BEV. The person who is competent in this CU should be able to carry out BEV scheduled maintenance workplace preparation, carry out BEV health check activities and carry out BEV maintenance activities. The outcome of this CU is the workplace preparation activities can be conducted in a safe manner and with an appropriate procedure in accordance with company safety and health policy.\n119\t\n120\t#### WA 1: Carry out BEV schedule maintenance workplace preparation\n121\t\n122\t| Work Steps | Performance Criteria |\n123\t|-----------|---------------------|\n124\t| 1.1 Identify licensing legislative, regulatory or certification requirement | 1.1 Licensing, legislative, regulatory or certification requirement determined in accordance with regulatory authority |\n125\t| 1.2 Identify BEV schedule maintenance safety & health requirement | 1.2 BEV workplace safety & health applied in accordance with manufacturer and company safety & health guideline |\n126\t| 1.3 Identify schedule maintenance tools and equipment | 1.3 Tools and equipment required for scheduled maintenance prepared in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n127\t| 1.4 Identify hazard in relation to working within a workshop environment | 1.4 Hazard in relation to working in workshop environment determined in accordance with company Job Hazard Analysis (JHA) checklist |\n128\t| 1.5 Identify scheduled waste management procedure | 1.5 Waste management procedure applied in accordance with environmental regulation guideline |\n129\t| 1.6 Arrange BEV scheduled maintenance workplace preparation | 1.6 BEV scheduled maintenance workplace preparation prepared in accordance with company standard operating procedure |\n130\t\n131\t#### WA 2: Carry out BEV health check activities\n132\t\n133\t| Work Steps | Performance Criteria |\n134\t|-----------|---------------------|\n135\t| 2.1 Identify BEV health check safety requirement | 2.1 BEV health check safety requirement applied in accordance with manufacturer's safety manual |\n136\t| 2.2 Identify BEV health check tools and equipment | 2.2 BEV health check tools and equipment prepared in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n137\t| 2.3 Execute BEV components active test | 2.3 BEV components active test checked in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n138\t| 2.4 Identify diagnostic fault code | 2.4 Fault code recorded in accordance with diagnostic result |\n139\t| 2.5 Prepare BEV health check report | 2.5 BEV health check report submitted and filed in accordance with company standard operating procedure |\n140\t\n141\t#### WA 3: Carry out BEV maintenance activities\n142\t\n143\t| Work Steps | Performance Criteria |\n144\t|-----------|---------------------|\n145\t| 3.1 Identify BEV maintenance safety requirement | 3.1 BEV maintenance activities safety requirement applied in accordance with manufacturer's safety manual |\n146\t| 3.2 Identify type of BEV and specification | 3.2 BEV manufacture, model, and specification determined in accordance with vehicle registration form |\n147\t| 3.3 Interpret BEV scheduled maintenance checklist | 3.3 BEV scheduled maintenance checklist confirmed in accordance with company standard operating procedure (SOP) |\n148\t| 3.4 Prepare BEV maintenance diagnostic tools and equipment | 3.4 BEV maintenance diagnostic tools and equipment setup in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n149\t| 3.5 Inspect BEV maintenance item | 3.5 BEV maintenance item rectified in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n150\t\n151\t### 6.2 CU C02: Perform Battery Management System (BMS) Rectification (G452-010-3:2023-C02)\n152\t\n153\t**CU Descriptor:** Perform Battery Management System (BMS) Rectification is the activities on how to rectify battery management system fault, which is diagnosis, interpret, inspect, and replace the battery pack in accordance with manufacturer's service manual. The person who is competent in this CU should be able to carry out BMS rectification workplace preparation, carry out BEV high voltage cable replacement, carry out BEV On-Board Charger (OBC) replacement, carry out BEV pre-charge control system servicing, carry out BEV traction battery pack replacement and carry out BEV DC-DC converter replacement. The outcome of this CU is able to define the BEV battery component and demonstrate suitable approached for BMS replacement in accordance with manufacturer's service manual.\n154\t\n155\t#### WA 1: Carry out Battery Management System (BMS) rectification workplace preparation\n156\t\n157\t| Work Steps | Performance Criteria |\n158\t|-----------|---------------------|\n159\t| 1.1 Identify licensing legislative, regulatory or certification requirement | 1.1 Licensing, legislative, regulatory or certification requirement determined in accordance with regulatory authority |\n160\t| 1.2 Identify BMS rectification safety and health requirements | 1.2 BMS rectification workplace preparation safety & health requirement applied in accordance with manufacturer and company safety & health guideline |\n161\t| 1.3 Identify BMS rectification high voltage tools and equipment requirement | 1.3 BMS rectification high voltage isolated tools & equipment prepared in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n162\t| 1.4 Identify BMS rectification hazard in relation to working within a workshop environment | 1.4 BMS rectification hazard in relation to working within a workshop environment identified in accordance with company Job Hazard Analysis Checklist |\n163\t| 1.5 Identify BMS rectification schedule waste management procedure | 1.5 BMS rectification scheduled waste management procedure applied in accordance with company safety, health and environment guideline |\n164\t| 1.6 Arrange BMS rectification workplace preparation | 1.6 BMS workplace rectification preparation prepared in accordance with company standard operating procedure |\n165\t\n166\t#### WA 2: Carry out BEV high voltage cable replacement\n167\t\n168\t| Work Steps | Performance Criteria |\n169\t|-----------|---------------------|\n170\t| 2.1 Identify BEV high voltage cable replacement safety requirements | 2.1 BEV high voltage cable replacement safety requirement applied in accordance with manufacturer and company safety manual |\n171\t| 2.2 Identify BEV high voltage cable replacement tools and equipment | 2.2 BEV high voltage cable replacement tools and lifting equipment prepared in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n172\t| 2.3 Disconnect BEV low voltage circuit | 2.3 BEV low voltage circuit disconnected in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n173\t| 2.4 Disconnect BEV high voltage circuit | 2.4 BEV high voltage circuit disconnected in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n174\t| 2.5 Inspect high voltage cable condition | 2.5 High voltage cable condition checked in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n175\t| 2.6 Inspect high voltage cable interlock connection | 2.6 High voltage cable interlock condition checked in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n176\t| 2.7 Change high voltage cable | 2.7 High voltage cable resistant and continuity replaced in accordance with diagnostic result |\n177\t| 2.8 Test high voltage system functionality | 2.8 High voltage system functionality confirmed in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n178\t\n179\t#### WA 3: Carry out BEV On-Board Charger (OBC) replacement\n180\t\n181\t| Work Steps | Performance Criteria |\n182\t|-----------|---------------------|\n183\t| 3.1 Identify BEV OBC safety requirements | 3.1 OBC replacement safety requirement applied in accordance with manufacturer's safety manual |\n184\t| 3.2 Identify BEV OBC high voltage cable replacement tools and equipment | 3.2 BEV OBC high voltage cable replacement tools and equipment prepared in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n185\t| 3.3 Inspect BEV OBC functionality | 3.3 BEV OBC functionality checked using diagnostic tools in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n186\t| 3.4 Disconnect BEV low voltage circuit | 3.4 BEV low voltage circuit detached in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n187\t| 3.5 Disconnect BEV high voltage circuit | 3.5 BEV high voltage circuit detached in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n188\t| 3.6 Drain out coolant from the high voltage system | 3.6 Coolant from the high voltage system emptied in accordance with manufacturer's service manual and waste management procedure |\n189\t| 3.7 Disconnect OBC high voltage cable | 3.7 High voltage cable from the OBC detached in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n190\t| 3.8 Diagnose BEV on-board charger functionality | 3.8 BEV OBC fault code identified in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n191\t| 3.9 Change BEV OBC | 3.9 New OBC installed in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n192\t| 3.10 Refill high voltage system coolant | 3.10 High voltage system coolant refill confirmed in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n193\t| 3.11 Test BEV OBC functionality | 3.11 BEV OBC functionality confirmed in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n194\t\n195\t#### WA 4: Carry out BEV pre-charge control system servicing\n196\t\n197\t| Work Steps | Performance Criteria |\n198\t|-----------|---------------------|\n199\t| 4.1 Identify BEV pre-charge relay pack safety requirements | 4.1 BEV pre-charge relay pack servicing safety requirement applied in accordance with manufacturer's safety manual |\n200\t| 4.2 Identify BEV high voltage cable replacement tools and equipment | 4.2 BEV high voltage cable replacement tools and equipment prepared in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n201\t| 4.3 Inspect BEV pre-charge control circuits | 4.3 BEV pre-charge control circuit functionality checked in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n202\t| 4.4 Disconnect BEV low voltage circuit | 4.4 BEV low voltage circuit disconnected in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n203\t| 4.5 Disconnect BEV high voltage circuit | 4.5 BEV high voltage circuit disconnected in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n204\t| 4.6 Diagnose pre-charge control circuits components | 4.6 Pre-charge control circuits component's fault code identified in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n205\t| 4.7 Change defective pre-charge relay pack | 4.7 Defective pre-charge relay pack replaced in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n206\t| 4.8 Test BEV pre-charge relay pack functionality | 4.8 BEV pre-charge relay pack functionality confirmed in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n207\t\n208\t#### WA 5: Carry out BEV traction battery pack replacement\n209\t\n210\t| Work Steps | Performance Criteria |\n211\t|-----------|---------------------|\n212\t| 5.1 Identify BEV traction battery pack replacement safety requirements | 5.1 BEV traction battery pack replacement safety requirement applied in accordance with manufacturer's safety manual |\n213\t| 5.2 Identify traction battery pack diagnosis & replacement tools and lifting equipment | 5.2 Tools and equipment for traction battery pack diagnosis & replacement prepared in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n214\t| 5.3 Inspect traction battery pack functionality | 5.3 Traction battery packed functionality checked using diagnostic tools in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n215\t| 5.4 Disconnect BEV low voltage circuit | 5.4 BEV low voltage circuit detached in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n216\t| 5.5 Disconnect BEV high voltage circuit | 5.5 BEV high voltage circuit detached in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n217\t| 5.6 Drain traction battery pack coolant or refrigerant | 5.6 Traction battery pack coolant or refrigerant emptied in accordance with manufacturer's service manual and waste management system |\n218\t| 5.7 Remove traction battery pack | 5.7 Traction battery pack detached from the vehicle in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n219\t| 5.8 Inspect battery module system | 5.8 Battery module system checked in accordance with manufacturer's specification |\n220\t| 5.9 Change faulty battery module unit | 5.9 Faulty battery module unit replaced in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n221\t| 5.10 Refill BEV traction battery coolant or refrigerant | 5.10 BEV traction battery coolant or refrigerant refill confirmed in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n222\t| 5.11 Test traction battery pack | 5.11 Traction battery pack condition and functionality confirmed in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n223\t\n224\t#### WA 6: Carry out BEV DC-DC converter replacement\n225\t\n226\t| Work Steps | Performance Criteria |\n227\t|-----------|---------------------|\n228\t| 6.1 Identify BEV DC-DC converter replacement safety requirement | 6.1 DC-DC converter replacement safety requirement applied in accordance with manufacturer's safety manual |\n229\t| 6.2 Identify DC-DC converter diagnosis & replacement tools and equipment | 6.2 Tools and equipment for DC-DC converter diagnosis & replacement prepared in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n230\t| 6.3 Inspect DC-DC converter's functionality | 6.3 DC-DC converter's functionality checked using diagnostic tools in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n231\t| 6.4 Diagnose BEV DC-DC converter | 6.4 BEV DC-DC converter fault code identified in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n232\t| 6.5 Drain DC-DC converter coolant | 6.5 DC-DC converter coolant emptied in accordance with manufacturer's service manual and waste management procedure |\n233\t| 6.6 Disconnect BEV low voltage circuit | 6.6 BEV low voltage circuit detached in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n234\t| 6.7 Disconnect BEV high voltage circuit | 6.7 BEV high voltage circuit detached in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n235\t| 6.8 Change faulty DC-DC converter | 6.8 Faulty DC-DC converter replaced in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n236\t| 6.9 Refill DC-DC converter coolant | 6.9 BEV DC-DC converter coolant refill confirmed in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n237\t| 6.10 Test DC-DC converter functionality | 6.10 DC-DC converter functionality confirmed in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n238\t\n239\t### 6.3 CU C03: Perform BEV Thermal Cooling System Rectification (G452-010-3:2023-C03)\n240\t\n241\t**CU Descriptor:** Perform BEV Thermal Cooling System Rectification is the activities to prepare working place, the method how to examine, rectified thermal cooling system, service liquid and air conditioning cooling system according to manufacturer's service manual. The person who is competent in this CU should be able to carry out thermal cooling system rectification workplace preparation, carry out BEV liquid cooling system servicing and carry out BEV HVAC system servicing. The outcome of this CU is the functionality of vehicle thermal cooling system can be restored and prevention of electric traction motor and batteries from over-heating in accordance with manufacturer's service manual.\n242\t\n243\t#### WA 1: Carry out thermal cooling system rectification workplace preparation\n244\t\n245\t| Work Steps | Performance Criteria |\n246\t|-----------|---------------------|\n247\t| 1.1 Identify licensing, legislative, regulatory or certificate requirement | 1.1 Licensing, legislative, regulatory or certification requirement determined in accordance with regulatory authority |\n248\t| 1.2 Identify BEV thermal cooling system rectification safety & health requirements | 1.2 BEV thermal cooling system rectification safety & health requirement applied in accordance with manufacturer and company safety & health guideline |\n249\t| 1.3 Identify thermal cooling system diagnosis & replacement tools and equipment | 1.3 Thermal cooling system rectification high voltage isolated tools & equipment prepared in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n250\t| 1.4 Identify thermal cooling system rectification hazard in relation to working within a workshop environment | 1.4 Thermal cooling system rectification hazard in relation to working within a workshop environment identified in accordance with company Job Hazard Analysis checklist |\n251\t| 1.5 Identify thermal cooling system rectification schedule waste management procedure | 1.5 Thermal cooling system rectification schedule waste management procedure applied in accordance with waste management guideline |\n252\t| 1.6 Arrange BEV thermal cooling system rectification workplace preparation | 1.6 BEV thermal cooling system rectification workplace preparation confirmed in accordance with company standard operating procedure |\n253\t\n254\t#### WA 2: Carry out BEV liquid cooling system servicing\n255\t\n256\t| Work Steps | Performance Criteria |\n257\t|-----------|---------------------|\n258\t| 2.1 Identify BEV liquid cooling servicing safety requirement | 2.1 BEV liquid cooling servicing safety requirements applied in accordance with manufacturer and company safety manual |\n259\t| 2.2 Identify liquid cooling diagnosis & servicing tools and equipment | 2.2 Liquid cooling diagnosis & servicing tools and equipment prepared in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n260\t| 2.3 Check cooling system electric water pumps condition | 2.3 Cooling system electric water pumps functionality verified in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n261\t| 2.4 Check cooling system for leakage, corrosion and clogging | 2.4 Cooling system for leakage and functionality verified in accordance with diagnostic tools result |\n262\t| 2.5 Change faulty cooling system component | 2.5 Faulty cooling system component replaced in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n263\t| 2.6 Test cooling system | 2.6 Cooling system functionality confirmed in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n264\t\n265\t#### WA 3: Carry out BEV Heating, Ventilation and Air Conditioning (HVAC) system servicing\n266\t\n267\t| Work Steps | Performance Criteria |\n268\t|-----------|---------------------|\n269\t| 3.1 Identify BEV HVAC system servicing safety requirements | 3.1 BEV HVAC system servicing safety requirements applied in accordance with manufacturer safety manual |\n270\t| 3.2 Identify HVAC system diagnosis & servicing tools and equipment | 3.2 HVAC system diagnosis & servicing tools and equipment prepared in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n271\t| 3.3 Check HVAC system | 3.3 HVAC system for leakage and functionality tested in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n272\t| 3.4 Replace faulty HVAC system component | 3.4 Faulty HVAC system component replaced in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n273\t| 3.5 Test HVAC system | 3.5 HVAC system functionality confirmed in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n274\t\n275\t### 6.4 CU C04: Perform BEV Electric Drive Train System Rectification (G452-010-3:2023-C04)\n276\t\n277\t**CU Descriptor:** Perform BEV Electric Drive Train System Rectification is the activities for the electric drive system replacement. From this activity the personnel will be exposed to the procedure on how to diagnose, inspect, replace faulty unit in accordance with manufacturer's service manual as well as to understand on the safety, health and environment requirement as per OSHA 1994 and EQA 1974. The person who is competent in this CU should be able to carry out electric drive train system rectification workplace preparation, carry out inverter unit replacement, carry out BEV traction motor replacement, carry out motorised transaxle servicing and carry out BEV integrated brake control system servicing. The outcome of this CU is able to define and demonstrate the process flow for the drive train delivering power from the battery to the wheels, also helps to keep the motor running at its most efficient speed in accordance with manufacturer service manual.\n278\t\n279\t#### WA 1: Carry out electric drive train system rectification workplace preparation\n280\t\n281\t| Work Steps | Performance Criteria |\n282\t|-----------|---------------------|\n283\t| 1.1 Identify licensing, legislative, regulatory or certificate requirement | 1.1 Licensing, legislative, regulatory or certification requirement determined in accordance with regulatory authority |\n284\t| 1.2 Identify BEV electric drive train rectification safety & health requirements | 1.2 BEV electric drive train rectification safety & health requirements applied in accordance with manufacturer and company safety & health guideline |\n285\t| 1.3 Identify electric drive train system rectification tools & equipment requirement | 1.3 Electric drive train system rectification tools & equipment requirement prepared in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n286\t| 1.4 Identify BEV electric drive train rectification hazard in relation to working within a workshop environment | 1.4 BEV electric drive train rectification hazard in relation to working within a workshop environment validated in accordance with company Job Hazard Analysis checklist |\n287\t| 1.5 Identify BEV electric drive train rectification schedule waste management procedure | 1.5 BEV electric drive train rectification schedule waste management procedure applied in accordance with company waste disposal guideline |\n288\t| 1.6 Arrange BEV electric drive train system rectification workplace preparation | 1.6 BEV electric drive train system rectification workplace preparation confirmed in accordance with company standard operating procedure |\n289\t\n290\t#### WA 2: Carry out BEV inverter unit replacement\n291\t\n292\t| Work Steps | Performance Criteria |\n293\t|-----------|---------------------|\n294\t| 2.1 Identify BEV inverter unit replacement safety | 2.1 BEV inverter unit replacement safety requirements applied in accordance with manufacturer's safety manual |\n295\t| 2.2 Identify inverter unit diagnosis and replacement tools and equipment | 2.2 Inverter unit diagnosis and replacement tools and equipment prepared in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n296\t| 2.3 Inspect inverter unit functionality | 2.3 Inverter unit functionality checked in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n297\t| 2.4 Diagnose inverter unit functionality | 2.4 Inverter unit fault code identified in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n298\t| 2.5 Disconnect BEV low voltage circuit | 2.5 BEV low voltage circuit disconnected in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n299\t| 2.6 Disconnect BEV high voltage circuit | 2.6 BEV high voltage circuit disconnected in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4aad63e8-74bd-4073-a099-f779e2e73025","uuid":"bf9d1b38-fe7b-464b-b10b-50de6e03323d","timestamp":"2026-04-16T14:55:52.399Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:/Users/Jyue/Documents/1-projects/noss-to-wim/bev-diagnostic-rectification/00-noss-extract.md","content":"| G452-010-3:2023-C04-W04 | Carry Out BEV Motorised Transaxle Servicing |\n| G452-010-3:2023-C04-W05 | Carry Out BEV Integrated Brake Control System Replacement |\n\n#### CU C05: Perform BEV Workshop Supervisory Function\n\n| WA Code | Work Activity |\n|---------|---------------|\n| G452-010-3:2023-C05-W01 | Conduct BEV Daily Briefing |\n| G452-010-3:2023-C05-W02 | Monitor BEV Subordinate's Performance |\n| G452-010-3:2023-C05-W03 | Coordinate BEV Subordinate's Training |\n| G452-010-3:2023-C05-W04 | Monitor BEV Workshop Safety, Health & Environment |\n\n---\n\n## 6. Competency Profile (CP)\n\n### 6.1 CU C01: Perform BEV Scheduled Maintenance (G452-010-3:2023-C01)\n\n**CU Descriptor:** Perform BEV Scheduled Maintenance preliminary is the first and necessary steps to set-up a working place. This is to determine the working environment for servicing and maintenance of BEV are equipped, safe and suitable. Check the function and condition of safety equipment and component of BEV. The person who is competent in this CU should be able to carry out BEV scheduled maintenance workplace preparation, carry out BEV health check activities and carry out BEV maintenance activities. The outcome of this CU is the workplace preparation activities can be conducted in a safe manner and with an appropriate procedure in accordance with company safety and health policy.\n\n#### WA 1: Carry out BEV schedule maintenance workplace preparation\n\n| Work Steps | Performance Criteria |\n|-----------|---------------------|\n| 1.1 Identify licensing legislative, regulatory or certification requirement | 1.1 Licensing, legislative, regulatory or certification requirement determined in accordance with regulatory authority |\n| 1.2 Identify BEV schedule maintenance safety & health requirement | 1.2 BEV workplace safety & health applied in accordance with manufacturer and company safety & health guideline |\n| 1.3 Identify schedule maintenance tools and equipment | 1.3 Tools and equipment required for scheduled maintenance prepared in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n| 1.4 Identify hazard in relation to working within a workshop environment | 1.4 Hazard in relation to working in workshop environment determined in accordance with company Job Hazard Analysis (JHA) checklist |\n| 1.5 Identify scheduled waste management procedure | 1.5 Waste management procedure applied in accordance with environmental regulation guideline |\n| 1.6 Arrange BEV scheduled maintenance workplace preparation | 1.6 BEV scheduled maintenance workplace preparation prepared in accordance with company standard operating procedure |\n\n#### WA 2: Carry out BEV health check activities\n\n| Work Steps | Performance Criteria |\n|-----------|---------------------|\n| 2.1 Identify BEV health check safety requirement | 2.1 BEV health check safety requirement applied in accordance with manufacturer's safety manual |\n| 2.2 Identify BEV health check tools and equipment | 2.2 BEV health check tools and equipment prepared in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n| 2.3 Execute BEV components active test | 2.3 BEV components active test checked in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n| 2.4 Identify diagnostic fault code | 2.4 Fault code recorded in accordance with diagnostic result |\n| 2.5 Prepare BEV health check report | 2.5 BEV health check report submitted and filed in accordance with company standard operating procedure |\n\n#### WA 3: Carry out BEV maintenance activities\n\n| Work Steps | Performance Criteria |\n|-----------|---------------------|\n| 3.1 Identify BEV maintenance safety requirement | 3.1 BEV maintenance activities safety requirement applied in accordance with manufacturer's safety manual |\n| 3.2 Identify type of BEV and specification | 3.2 BEV manufacture, model, and specification determined in accordance with vehicle registration form |\n| 3.3 Interpret BEV scheduled maintenance checklist | 3.3 BEV scheduled maintenance checklist confirmed in accordance with company standard operating procedure (SOP) |\n| 3.4 Prepare BEV maintenance diagnostic tools and equipment | 3.4 BEV maintenance diagnostic tools and equipment setup in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n| 3.5 Inspect BEV maintenance item | 3.5 BEV maintenance item rectified in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n\n### 6.2 CU C02: Perform Battery Management System (BMS) Rectification (G452-010-3:2023-C02)\n\n**CU Descriptor:** Perform Battery Management System (BMS) Rectification is the activities on how to rectify battery management system fault, which is diagnosis, interpret, inspect, and replace the battery pack in accordance with manufacturer's service manual. The person who is competent in this CU should be able to carry out BMS rectification workplace preparation, carry out BEV high voltage cable replacement, carry out BEV On-Board Charger (OBC) replacement, carry out BEV pre-charge control system servicing, carry out BEV traction battery pack replacement and carry out BEV DC-DC converter replacement. The outcome of this CU is able to define the BEV battery component and demonstrate suitable approached for BMS replacement in accordance with manufacturer's service manual.\n\n#### WA 1: Carry out Battery Management System (BMS) rectification workplace preparation\n\n| Work Steps | Performance Criteria |\n|-----------|---------------------|\n| 1.1 Identify licensing legislative, regulatory or certification requirement | 1.1 Licensing, legislative, regulatory or certification requirement determined in accordance with regulatory authority |\n| 1.2 Identify BMS rectification safety and health requirements | 1.2 BMS rectification workplace preparation safety & health requirement applied in accordance with manufacturer and company safety & health guideline |\n| 1.3 Identify BMS rectification high voltage tools and equipment requirement | 1.3 BMS rectification high voltage isolated tools & equipment prepared in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n| 1.4 Identify BMS rectification hazard in relation to working within a workshop environment | 1.4 BMS rectification hazard in relation to working within a workshop environment identified in accordance with company Job Hazard Analysis Checklist |\n| 1.5 Identify BMS rectification schedule waste management procedure | 1.5 BMS rectification scheduled waste management procedure applied in accordance with company safety, health and environment guideline |\n| 1.6 Arrange BMS rectification workplace preparation | 1.6 BMS workplace rectification preparation prepared in accordance with company standard operating procedure |\n\n#### WA 2: Carry out BEV high voltage cable replacement\n\n| Work Steps | Performance Criteria |\n|-----------|---------------------|\n| 2.1 Identify BEV high voltage cable replacement safety requirements | 2.1 BEV high voltage cable replacement safety requirement applied in accordance with manufacturer and company safety manual |\n| 2.2 Identify BEV high voltage cable replacement tools and equipment | 2.2 BEV high voltage cable replacement tools and lifting equipment prepared in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n| 2.3 Disconnect BEV low voltage circuit | 2.3 BEV low voltage circuit disconnected in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n| 2.4 Disconnect BEV high voltage circuit | 2.4 BEV high voltage circuit disconnected in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n| 2.5 Inspect high voltage cable condition | 2.5 High voltage cable condition checked in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n| 2.6 Inspect high voltage cable interlock connection | 2.6 High voltage cable interlock condition checked in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n| 2.7 Change high voltage cable | 2.7 High voltage cable resistant and continuity replaced in accordance with diagnostic result |\n| 2.8 Test high voltage system functionality | 2.8 High voltage system functionality confirmed in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n\n#### WA 3: Carry out BEV On-Board Charger (OBC) replacement\n\n| Work Steps | Performance Criteria |\n|-----------|---------------------|\n| 3.1 Identify BEV OBC safety requirements | 3.1 OBC replacement safety requirement applied in accordance with manufacturer's safety manual |\n| 3.2 Identify BEV OBC high voltage cable replacement tools and equipment | 3.2 BEV OBC high voltage cable replacement tools and equipment prepared in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n| 3.3 Inspect BEV OBC functionality | 3.3 BEV OBC functionality checked using diagnostic tools in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n| 3.4 Disconnect BEV low voltage circuit | 3.4 BEV low voltage circuit detached in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n| 3.5 Disconnect BEV high voltage circuit | 3.5 BEV high voltage circuit detached in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n| 3.6 Drain out coolant from the high voltage system | 3.6 Coolant from the high voltage system emptied in accordance with manufacturer's service manual and waste management procedure |\n| 3.7 Disconnect OBC high voltage cable | 3.7 High voltage cable from the OBC detached in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n| 3.8 Diagnose BEV on-board charger functionality | 3.8 BEV OBC fault code identified in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n| 3.9 Change BEV OBC | 3.9 New OBC installed in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n| 3.10 Refill high voltage system coolant | 3.10 High voltage system coolant refill confirmed in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n| 3.11 Test BEV OBC functionality | 3.11 BEV OBC functionality confirmed in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n\n#### WA 4: Carry out BEV pre-charge control system servicing\n\n| Work Steps | Performance Criteria |\n|-----------|---------------------|\n| 4.1 Identify BEV pre-charge relay pack safety requirements | 4.1 BEV pre-charge relay pack servicing safety requirement applied in accordance with manufacturer's safety manual |\n| 4.2 Identify BEV high voltage cable replacement tools and equipment | 4.2 BEV high voltage cable replacement tools and equipment prepared in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n| 4.3 Inspect BEV pre-charge control circuits | 4.3 BEV pre-charge control circuit functionality checked in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n| 4.4 Disconnect BEV low voltage circuit | 4.4 BEV low voltage circuit disconnected in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n| 4.5 Disconnect BEV high voltage circuit | 4.5 BEV high voltage circuit disconnected in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n| 4.6 Diagnose pre-charge control circuits components | 4.6 Pre-charge control circuits component's fault code identified in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n| 4.7 Change defective pre-charge relay pack | 4.7 Defective pre-charge relay pack replaced in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n| 4.8 Test BEV pre-charge relay pack functionality | 4.8 BEV pre-charge relay pack functionality confirmed in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n\n#### WA 5: Carry out BEV traction battery pack replacement\n\n| Work Steps | Performance Criteria |\n|-----------|---------------------|\n| 5.1 Identify BEV traction battery pack replacement safety requirements | 5.1 BEV traction battery pack replacement safety requirement applied in accordance with manufacturer's safety manual |\n| 5.2 Identify traction battery pack diagnosis & replacement tools and lifting equipment | 5.2 Tools and equipment for traction battery pack diagnosis & replacement prepared in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n| 5.3 Inspect traction battery pack functionality | 5.3 Traction battery packed functionality checked using diagnostic tools in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n| 5.4 Disconnect BEV low voltage circuit | 5.4 BEV low voltage circuit detached in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n| 5.5 Disconnect BEV high voltage circuit | 5.5 BEV high voltage circuit detached in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n| 5.6 Drain traction battery pack coolant or refrigerant | 5.6 Traction battery pack coolant or refrigerant emptied in accordance with manufacturer's service manual and waste management system |\n| 5.7 Remove traction battery pack | 5.7 Traction battery pack detached from the vehicle in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n| 5.8 Inspect battery module system | 5.8 Battery module system checked in accordance with manufacturer's specification |\n| 5.9 Change faulty battery module unit | 5.9 Faulty battery module unit replaced in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n| 5.10 Refill BEV traction battery coolant or refrigerant | 5.10 BEV traction battery coolant or refrigerant refill confirmed in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n| 5.11 Test traction battery pack | 5.11 Traction battery pack condition and functionality confirmed in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n\n#### WA 6: Carry out BEV DC-DC converter replacement\n\n| Work Steps | Performance Criteria |\n|-----------|---------------------|\n| 6.1 Identify BEV DC-DC converter replacement safety requirement | 6.1 DC-DC converter replacement safety requirement applied in accordance with manufacturer's safety manual |\n| 6.2 Identify DC-DC converter diagnosis & replacement tools and equipment | 6.2 Tools and equipment for DC-DC converter diagnosis & replacement prepared in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n| 6.3 Inspect DC-DC converter's functionality | 6.3 DC-DC converter's functionality checked using diagnostic tools in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n| 6.4 Diagnose BEV DC-DC converter | 6.4 BEV DC-DC converter fault code identified in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n| 6.5 Drain DC-DC converter coolant | 6.5 DC-DC converter coolant emptied in accordance with manufacturer's service manual and waste management procedure |\n| 6.6 Disconnect BEV low voltage circuit | 6.6 BEV low voltage circuit detached in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n| 6.7 Disconnect BEV high voltage circuit | 6.7 BEV high voltage circuit detached in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n| 6.8 Change faulty DC-DC converter | 6.8 Faulty DC-DC converter replaced in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n| 6.9 Refill DC-DC converter coolant | 6.9 BEV DC-DC converter coolant refill confirmed in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n| 6.10 Test DC-DC converter functionality | 6.10 DC-DC converter functionality confirmed in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n\n### 6.3 CU C03: Perform BEV Thermal Cooling System Rectification (G452-010-3:2023-C03)\n\n**CU Descriptor:** Perform BEV Thermal Cooling System Rectification is the activities to prepare working place, the method how to examine, rectified thermal cooling system, service liquid and air conditioning cooling system according to manufacturer's service manual. The person who is competent in this CU should be able to carry out thermal cooling system rectification workplace preparation, carry out BEV liquid cooling system servicing and carry out BEV HVAC system servicing. The outcome of this CU is the functionality of vehicle thermal cooling system can be restored and prevention of electric traction motor and batteries from over-heating in accordance with manufacturer's service manual.\n\n#### WA 1: Carry out thermal cooling system rectification workplace preparation\n\n| Work Steps | Performance Criteria |\n|-----------|---------------------|\n| 1.1 Identify licensing, legislative, regulatory or certificate requirement | 1.1 Licensing, legislative, regulatory or certification requirement determined in accordance with regulatory authority |\n| 1.2 Identify BEV thermal cooling system rectification safety & health requirements | 1.2 BEV thermal cooling system rectification safety & health requirement applied in accordance with manufacturer and company safety & health guideline |\n| 1.3 Identify thermal cooling system diagnosis & replacement tools and equipment | 1.3 Thermal cooling system rectification high voltage isolated tools & equipment prepared in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n| 1.4 Identify thermal cooling system rectification hazard in relation to working within a workshop environment | 1.4 Thermal cooling system rectification hazard in relation to working within a workshop environment identified in accordance with company Job Hazard Analysis checklist |\n| 1.5 Identify thermal cooling system rectification schedule waste management procedure | 1.5 Thermal cooling system rectification schedule waste management procedure applied in accordance with waste management guideline |\n| 1.6 Arrange BEV thermal cooling system rectification workplace preparation | 1.6 BEV thermal cooling system rectification workplace preparation confirmed in accordance with company standard operating procedure |\n\n#### WA 2: Carry out BEV liquid cooling system servicing\n\n| Work Steps | Performance Criteria |\n|-----------|---------------------|\n| 2.1 Identify BEV liquid cooling servicing safety requirement | 2.1 BEV liquid cooling servicing safety requirements applied in accordance with manufacturer and company safety manual |\n| 2.2 Identify liquid cooling diagnosis & servicing tools and equipment | 2.2 Liquid cooling diagnosis & servicing tools and equipment prepared in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n| 2.3 Check cooling system electric water pumps condition | 2.3 Cooling system electric water pumps functionality verified in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n| 2.4 Check cooling system for leakage, corrosion and clogging | 2.4 Cooling system for leakage and functionality verified in accordance with diagnostic tools result |\n| 2.5 Change faulty cooling system component | 2.5 Faulty cooling system component replaced in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n| 2.6 Test cooling system | 2.6 Cooling system functionality confirmed in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n\n#### WA 3: Carry out BEV Heating, Ventilation and Air Conditioning (HVAC) system servicing\n\n| Work Steps | Performance Criteria |\n|-----------|---------------------|\n| 3.1 Identify BEV HVAC system servicing safety requirements | 3.1 BEV HVAC system servicing safety requirements applied in accordance with manufacturer safety manual |\n| 3.2 Identify HVAC system diagnosis & servicing tools and equipment | 3.2 HVAC system diagnosis & servicing tools and equipment prepared in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n| 3.3 Check HVAC system | 3.3 HVAC system for leakage and functionality tested in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n| 3.4 Replace faulty HVAC system component | 3.4 Faulty HVAC system component replaced in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n| 3.5 Test HVAC system | 3.5 HVAC system functionality confirmed in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n\n### 6.4 CU C04: Perform BEV Electric Drive Train System Rectification (G452-010-3:2023-C04)\n\n**CU Descriptor:** Perform BEV Electric Drive Train System Rectification is the activities for the electric drive system replacement. From this activity the personnel will be exposed to the procedure on how to diagnose, inspect, replace faulty unit in accordance with manufacturer's service manual as well as to understand on the safety, health and environment requirement as per OSHA 1994 and EQA 1974. The person who is competent in this CU should be able to carry out electric drive train system rectification workplace preparation, carry out inverter unit replacement, carry out BEV traction motor replacement, carry out motorised transaxle servicing and carry out BEV integrated brake control system servicing. The outcome of this CU is able to define and demonstrate the process flow for the drive train delivering power from the battery to the wheels, also helps to keep the motor running at its most efficient speed in accordance with manufacturer service manual.\n\n#### WA 1: Carry out electric drive train system rectification workplace preparation\n\n| Work Steps | Performance Criteria |\n|-----------|---------------------|\n| 1.1 Identify licensing, legislative, regulatory or certificate requirement | 1.1 Licensing, legislative, regulatory or certification requirement determined in accordance with regulatory authority |\n| 1.2 Identify BEV electric drive train rectification safety & health requirements | 1.2 BEV electric drive train rectification safety & health requirements applied in accordance with manufacturer and company safety & health guideline |\n| 1.3 Identify electric drive train system rectification tools & equipment requirement | 1.3 Electric drive train system rectification tools & equipment requirement prepared in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n| 1.4 Identify BEV electric drive train rectification hazard in relation to working within a workshop environment | 1.4 BEV electric drive train rectification hazard in relation to working within a workshop environment validated in accordance with company Job Hazard Analysis checklist |\n| 1.5 Identify BEV electric drive train rectification schedule waste management procedure | 1.5 BEV electric drive train rectification schedule waste management procedure applied in accordance with company waste disposal guideline |\n| 1.6 Arrange BEV electric drive train system rectification workplace preparation | 1.6 BEV electric drive train system rectification workplace preparation confirmed in accordance with company standard operating procedure |\n\n#### WA 2: Carry out BEV inverter unit replacement\n\n| Work Steps | Performance Criteria |\n|-----------|---------------------|\n| 2.1 Identify BEV inverter unit replacement safety | 2.1 BEV inverter unit replacement safety requirements applied in accordance with manufacturer's safety manual |\n| 2.2 Identify inverter unit diagnosis and replacement tools and equipment | 2.2 Inverter unit diagnosis and replacement tools and equipment prepared in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n| 2.3 Inspect inverter unit functionality | 2.3 Inverter unit functionality checked in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n| 2.4 Diagnose inverter unit functionality | 2.4 Inverter unit fault code identified in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n| 2.5 Disconnect BEV low voltage circuit | 2.5 BEV low voltage circuit disconnected in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |\n| 2.6 Disconnect BEV high voltage circuit | 2.6 BEV high voltage circuit disconnected in accordance with manufacturer's service manual |","numLines":200,"startLine":100,"totalLines":962}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01Dm9t9CHMzynos9owBD7FwU","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Good. Now I have the full CoCU codes for C02. The work steps are:\n- C02.1.x: BMS Rectification Workplace Preparation\n- C02.2.x: HV Cable Replacement\n- C02.3.x: OBC Replacement\n- C02.4.x: Pre-charge Control System Servicing\n- C02.5.x: Traction Battery Pack Replacement\n- C02.6.x: DC-DC Converter Replacement\n\nNow I have all the information I need. Let me write the diagnosis block and plan, then implement.\n\n\nI can see that C01 already has a complete Q-Format section with 35 questions—the first 25 have CoCU references and a marking scheme, while the last 10 are enrichment scenarios without answers. C02, on the other hand, only has the assessment sections A through C with no Q-Format content yet, so I need to build that out to match the structure.\n\nThe issue is that C01's scenario questions fall short of the 30% threshold at 14.3%, so I need to add sample answers to Q26-Q35. For C02, I'm looking at creating a full Q-Format section from scratch with 40 questions, making sure to include CoCU references, a 25-mark answer key, and scenario questions with sample answers to meet that 30% requirement.\n\nI'll be modifying both files—adding the missing answers to C01's enrichment scenarios and building out C02's entire Q-Format section with all the necessary components.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":25773,"cache_read_input_tokens":52162,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":25773},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4aad63e8-74bd-4073-a099-f779e2e73025","uuid":"dfb15742-2c5c-4f86-8790-2c7b8fbeae20"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01Dm9t9CHMzynos9owBD7FwU","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now I have everything needed. Let me output the diagnosis block and plan.\n\n## Current State\n- `C01/KA.md`: Has 35-question Q-Format bank (Q1-Q35). Q1-Q25 have CoCU refs and 25-mark answer key. Q26-Q35 are \"Enrichment Practice Scenarios\" **with no sample answers** — only 5 scenarios (Q21-Q25) have 50+ word answers = 14.3%, below 30% threshold.\n- `C02/KA.md`: Has only Sections A-C (~18 questions total, no CoCU refs). **No Q-Format bank at all.** Needs 40+ questions.\n\n## Problem Identified\n1. C01 needs sample answers added to Q26-Q35 to reach ≥30% scenario coverage (15/35 = 42.9%)\n2. C02 needs a complete Q-Format section appended: 40 questions, BMS/HV Cable/OBC distribution, CoCU refs, 25-mark answer key, and Q21-Q32 (12 scenario questions) with 50+ word sample answers\n\n## Planned Changes\n- Modify `bev-diagnostic-rectification/C01/KA.md`: Insert `*Jawapan Contoh / Sample Answer:*` blocks after Q26-Q35\n- Modify `bev-diagnostic-rectification/C02/KA.md`: Append Q-Format section (Q1-Q40) with MCQ, graded scenarios with sample answers, and enrichment questions\n\n<plan>\n{\n  \"files_to_create\": [],\n  \"files_to_modify\": [\n    \"bev-diagnostic-rectification/C01/KA.md\",\n    \"bev-diagnostic-rectification/C02/KA.md\"\n  ],\n  \"functions_to_add\": [],\n  \"estimated_loc\": 250\n}\n</plan>\n\nNow executing. First, adding sample answers to C01/KA.md Q26-Q35:"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":25773,"cache_read_input_tokens":52162,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":25773},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4aad63e8-74bd-4073-a099-f779e2e73025","uuid":"5be16c4f-7247-4a8d-b19a-d3c595f241a8"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01Dm9t9CHMzynos9owBD7FwU","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01BxJaojefepBUxmBe4C99ux","name":"Edit","input":{"replace_all":false,"file_path":"C:/Users/Jyue/Documents/1-projects/noss-to-wim/bev-diagnostic-rectification/C01/KA.md","old_string":"**Q26.** Describe the correct sequence for donning HV PPE before commencing BEV HV circuit work, including the order each item is put on. [CoCU C01.1.2]\n**Q27.** Explain the difference between a hazard and a risk in the BEV workshop context. Provide one BEV-specific example of each. [CoCU C01.1.4]\n**Q28.** Explain what happens electrically if the pre-charge circuit fails and why this causes damage to the inverter. [CoCU C01.2.3]\n**Q29.** A flood-damaged BEV is delivered to your workshop. List the unique hazards and the special handling protocol required before any work begins. [CoCU C01.1.4 / C01.1.6]\n**Q30.** Explain the three-point zero-energy verification procedure for a BEV using a CAT III — 1000V multimeter. [CoCU C01.1.2]\n**Q31.** Describe the Hierarchy of Controls. Give one BEV HV-specific example of a control at each level. [CoCU C01.1.4]\n**Q32.** At what SOH percentage is traction battery pack replacement typically recommended? Explain why the BMS limits performance before this threshold. [CoCU C01.2.3]\n**Q33.** Describe how to identify a BEV's HV system voltage before PPE selection. Why does the system voltage affect PPE class choice? [CoCU C01.3.2]\n**Q34.** A BEV owner reports gradual range reduction over 2 years. List three battery-related causes a technician should investigate during a health check. [CoCU C01.2.3 / C01.2.4]\n**Q35.** State the specific regulatory obligations for disposing of BEV HV glycol coolant and 12V lead-acid batteries, including relevant legislation. [CoCU C01.1.5]","new_string":"**Q26.** Describe the correct sequence for donning HV PPE before commencing BEV HV circuit work, including the order each item is put on. [CoCU C01.1.2]\n\n*Jawapan Contoh / Sample Answer:* Correct donning sequence: (1) Safety boots — foot protection before all else. (2) Arc-rated overalls or suit over all clothing. (3) Safety glasses and face shield. (4) Class 0 or Class 1 insulated gloves — put on LAST, inspected with roll-cuff squeeze test first. Gloves must overlap suit sleeves. Removal order reverses: gloves first, then face shield, then suit. Never touch the outer surface of insulated gloves with bare hands after donning. Inspect cuff markings remain legible before each use.\n\n**Q27.** Explain the difference between a hazard and a risk in the BEV workshop context. Provide one BEV-specific example of each. [CoCU C01.1.4]\n\n*Jawapan Contoh / Sample Answer:* A hazard is a source with potential to cause harm — it exists independently of anyone being present. A risk is the probability that the hazard will cause harm, combined with the severity of that harm. BEV hazard example: an orange HV cable with visibly cracked insulation running beneath the battery pack — the damaged insulation is the hazard. BEV risk example: a technician reaching under the pack to retrieve a dropped tool without de-energising first — the probability (high if distracted) multiplied by severity (electrocution/fatal) = HIGH risk requiring immediate engineering or administrative control before work proceeds.\n\n**Q28.** Explain what happens electrically if the pre-charge circuit fails and why this causes damage to the inverter. [CoCU C01.2.3]\n\n*Jawapan Contoh / Sample Answer:* If the pre-charge circuit fails, the main positive contactor closes directly against uncharged inverter DC-link capacitors. Since capacitors at 0V present near-zero impedance, the battery pack discharges into them in an instantaneous current surge of hundreds of amperes. This inrush current: (1) arc-welds the main contactor contacts in the closed position (weld failure — contactor can no longer open), permanently connecting the battery to the HV bus; (2) may destroy the inverter's IGBT power transistors and capacitor dielectric through over-current stress. The pre-charge resistor (typically 50-200 Ω) prevents this by limiting inrush and allowing capacitors to charge gradually over 0.5-2 seconds before the main contactor engages.\n\n**Q29.** A flood-damaged BEV is delivered to your workshop. List the unique hazards and the special handling protocol required before any work begins. [CoCU C01.1.4 / C01.1.6]\n\n*Jawapan Contoh / Sample Answer:* Unique hazards: (1) Submerged cells may have internal micro-short circuits causing delayed thermal runaway hours or days after recovery. (2) Moisture inside battery housing creates unpredictable HV-to-chassis leakage paths not detected by standard IMD. (3) HV connector seals may be compromised, allowing water ingress. (4) Coolant and electrolyte may have mixed with contaminated floodwater — unknown chemical hazard. Special protocol: do NOT attempt READY ON under any circumstances. Park in an isolated open area at least 10 m from structures and other vehicles. Post continuous fire watch for minimum 48 hours. Do NOT open battery housing without specialist equipment. Notify the manufacturer's emergency technical hotline and document with photographs before any handling. Inform the workshop Safety and Health Officer before proceeding.\n\n**Q30.** Explain the three-point zero-energy verification procedure for a BEV using a CAT III — 1000V multimeter. [CoCU C01.1.2]\n\n*Jawapan Contoh / Sample Answer:* Step 1: Before testing the BEV, verify multimeter function by testing on a confirmed live LV circuit — confirms meter leads and fuses are functional. Step 2: After full HV de-energisation and 5-10 minute capacitor discharge wait, set meter to DC Voltage, 1000V range. Measure POINT 1: HV junction box main positive terminal to chassis ground — must read 0V DC. Step 3: Measure POINT 2: HV junction box main negative terminal to chassis ground — must read 0V DC. Step 4: Measure POINT 3: Battery pack positive terminal (accessed via service port) to chassis ground — must read 0V DC. All three points must confirm zero voltage before touching any HV component. Record readings on the work order before proceeding.\n\n**Q31.** Describe the Hierarchy of Controls. Give one BEV HV-specific example of a control at each level. [CoCU C01.1.4]\n\n*Jawapan Contoh / Sample Answer:* Five levels, most to least effective: (1) Elimination — remove the hazard entirely. BEV example: permanently decommission and dispose of a thermally compromised battery pack rather than attempting repair. (2) Substitution — replace with a less hazardous alternative. BEV example: use a wireless HV immobiliser system instead of manual cable disconnection for certain diagnostic operations. (3) Engineering controls — physically isolate the hazard. BEV example: automatic interlock that opens main contactors and energises warning lights when the battery housing is opened. (4) Administrative controls — change work procedures. BEV example: two-person rule requiring a second qualified technician to be present during all HV disconnection work. (5) PPE — last resort only. BEV example: Class 0 insulated gloves, arc-rated suit, and face shield for any HV component contact.\n\n**Q32.** At what SOH percentage is traction battery pack replacement typically recommended? Explain why the BMS limits performance before this threshold. [CoCU C01.2.3]\n\n*Jawapan Contoh / Sample Answer:* Pack replacement is typically recommended when SOH drops below 80%. Before this threshold, the BMS progressively limits performance to protect degraded cells: charge current is reduced to prevent voltage overshoot in weaker cells; discharge cut-off voltage is raised to prevent over-discharge of the lowest-capacity cells; available energy (usable kWh) is restricted to the capacity of the weakest cell in the series string, reducing range; regenerative braking current is reduced. These limitations protect the weakest cells from thermal runaway caused by over-charge or over-discharge stress, but they signal to the driver that pack replacement is approaching. Below 70% SOH, restrictions become severe enough to significantly impair daily usability.\n\n**Q33.** Describe how to identify a BEV's HV system voltage before PPE selection. Why does the system voltage affect PPE class choice? [CoCU C01.3.2]\n\n*Jawapan Contoh / Sample Answer:* Identification steps: (1) Check the manufacturer's workshop manual or Technical Service Bulletin using the vehicle VIN to confirm nominal HV system voltage (commonly 400V DC or 800V DC in current models). (2) Locate the HV warning label on the battery pack housing — Malaysian regulatory requirements specify that operating voltage must be displayed. (3) If uncertain, use a non-contact voltage detector at a safe distance to confirm HV presence before close approach. PPE class selection is affected because: Class 0 insulated gloves are proof-tested to 1000V and rated for use up to 500V AC / 750V DC systems. Class 1 gloves (tested to 7500V) should be used for 800V DC systems where transient overvoltage could exceed Class 0 limits. The wrong PPE class provides insufficient arc energy protection for the actual system voltage.\n\n**Q34.** A BEV owner reports gradual range reduction over 2 years. List three battery-related causes a technician should investigate during a health check. [CoCU C01.2.3 / C01.2.4]\n\n*Jawapan Contoh / Sample Answer:* Three causes to investigate: (1) Cell capacity degradation — cyclic and calendar aging reduce individual cell Ah capacity. Measure each cell's SOH via BMS diagnostic data; SOH below 80% confirms significant capacity loss as the primary cause. (2) Cell imbalance — one or more weak cells limit the entire pack's usable capacity. The BMS stops discharge when the weakest cell reaches cut-off voltage even though other cells remain charged. Identify by reviewing individual cell voltage spread at end-of-discharge in the diagnostic report — deviations exceeding ±0.05V indicate imbalance. (3) Increased internal resistance from electrolyte decomposition — causes greater voltage sag under load, reducing usable energy without obvious SOH change. Identify by comparing cell internal resistance measurements against baseline values in the manufacturer specification table.\n\n**Q35.** State the specific regulatory obligations for disposing of BEV HV glycol coolant and 12V lead-acid batteries, including relevant legislation. [CoCU C01.1.5]\n\n*Jawapan Contoh / Sample Answer:* HV glycol coolant — classified as Scheduled Waste SW305 under the Environmental Quality (Scheduled Wastes) Regulations 2005 made under the Environmental Quality Act 1974 (Act 127). Must be stored in sealed, labelled containers; cannot be disposed via drain, soil, or municipal waste. Must be consigned to a DOE-licensed Scheduled Waste contractor using the prescribed waste manifest form. Workshop must retain manifest records for minimum 3 years. 12V lead-acid batteries — classified as Scheduled Waste SW102 (used lead-acid batteries) under the same Regulations. Must be returned to licensed lead recyclers or manufacturer take-back programmes. Never crush, incinerate, or dispose in landfill. Penalty for improper disposal: up to RM500,000 fine and/or 5 years imprisonment under EQA 1974 Section 34B."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":25773,"cache_read_input_tokens":52162,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":25773},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4aad63e8-74bd-4073-a099-f779e2e73025","uuid":"f01a8359-8837-4996-9863-7efd486fe270"}
